window.CMA_QS=[{"id":"v1-su2-q218","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":1,"question":"1) The following information applies to a manufacturing company,\nwhich has a 6-month operating cycle:\nCash sales $100,000\nCredit sales during the sixth month with net 30 days\nterms 150,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable\nCredit sale during the fifth month with special terms\nof net 9 months 10,000\nInterest earned and accrued on an investment that\nmatures during month 3 of the next cycle 2,000\nThe total of the company’s trade accounts receivable at the\nend of the current cycle is","options":["$152,000","$160,000","$260,000","$262,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A receivable classified as current on the statement of financial\nposition is expected to be collected within the current\noperating cycle or 1 year, whichever is longer. The total of the\ntrade accounts receivable at the end of the current cycle is\ntherefore $160,000 ($150,000 + $10,000).","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q219","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":2,"question":"2) A company uses the allowance method to account for\nuncollectible accounts receivable. After recording the estimate\nof credit loss expense for the current year, the company\ndecided to write off in the current year the $10,000 account of\na customer who had filed for bankruptcy. What effect does this\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable\nwrite-off have on the company’s current net income and total\ncurrent assets, respectively?\nNet Income Total Current Assets","options":["Decrease Decrease","No effect Decrease","Decrease No effect","No effect No effect"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The company uses the allowance method. Thus, when a\nspecific amount is written off, the entry reduces the allowance\nfor credit losses and reduces accounts receivable. The net\neffect is no change to total current assets. The write-off of a\nparticular account has no effect on expenses, net income, and\ntotal current assets.","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q220","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":3,"question":"3) A company had the following account balances at\nDecember 31:\nAccounts receivable $ 900,000\nAllowance for credit losses (before any provision for\nthe year credit loss expense) 16,000\nCredit sales for the year 1,750,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable\nThe company is considering the following methods of\nestimating credit loss expense for the year:\n Based on credit sales at 2%\n Based on accounts receivable at 5%\nWhat amount should the company charge to credit loss\nexpense under each method?\nPercentage of\nCredit Sales\nPercentage of\nAccounts Receivable","options":["$51,000 $45,000","$51,000 $29,000","$35,000 $45,000","$35,000 $29,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Credit loss expense is estimated in two ways. One\nemphasizes asset valuation, while the other emphasizes\nincome measurement. The first is based on an aging of the\nreceivables to determine the balance in the allowance for\ncredit losses. Credit loss expense is the amount necessary to\nadjust the allowance account to this estimated balance. The\nsecond recognizes credit loss expense as a percentage of\nsales. Under the first method, if credit losses are estimated to\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable\nbe 5% of gross accounts receivable, the allowance account\nshould have a balance of $45,000 ($900,000 × 5%), and the\nentry is to credit loss expense and the allowance for $29,000\n($45,000 – $16,000 existing balance). Under the second\nmethod, credit loss expense is $35,000 ($1,750,000 × 2%).","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q221","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":4,"question":"4) Based on the industry average, a corporation estimates that its\ncredit losses should average 3% of credit sales. The balance\nin the allowance for credit losses at the beginning of Year 3\nwas $140,000. During Year 3, credit sales totaled\n$10,000,000, accounts of $100,000 were deemed to be\nuncollectible, and payment was received on a $20,000 account\nthat had previously been written off as uncollectible. The entry\nto record credit loss expense at the end of Year 3 would\ninclude an increase to the allowance for credit losses of","options":["$300,000","$260,000","$240,000","$160,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Credit loss expense is based on the income statement\napproach. It treats credit loss expense as a function of sales\non account. Thus, it is projected to be $300,000 ($10,000,000\n× 3%). The entry to record credit loss increases the balance of\nthe allowance for credit losses. Total assets decrease by\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable\n$300,000 because the allowance for credit loss account is a\ncontra asset. On the other side of the entry, stockholders’\nequity decreases by the amount of the credit loss expense.","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q222","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":5,"question":"5) The following information has been compiled by a\nmanufacturing company:\n Sale of company products for the period to customers\nwith net 30-day terms amounting to $150,000.\n Sale of company products for the period to a\ncustomer, supported by a note for $25,000, with\nspecial terms of net 180 days.\n Balance of trade receivables at the end of the last\nperiod was $300,000.\n Collections of open trade receivables during the period\nwas $200,000.\n Rental income for the period, both earned and accrued\nbut not yet collected, from the manufacturing\ncompany’s credit union for use of company facilities\nwas $2,000.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable\nThe open trade receivables balance to be shown on the\nstatement of financial position for the period is","options":["$250,000","$252,000","$275,000","$277,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The open trade receivables balance is calculated as follows:\nPrevious ending balance $300,000\nAdd: Sales to customers (terms net 30) 150,000\nMinus: Collections during period (200,000)\nOpen trade receivables reported $250,000","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q223","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":6,"question":"6) The following information relates to accounts receivable for the\nyear just ended:\nAccounts receivable, 1/1 $ 650,000\nCredit sales for the year 2,700,000\nSales returns for the year 75,000\nAccounts written off during the year 40,000\nCollections from customers during the year 2,150,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable\nEstimated future sales returns at 12/31 50,000\nEstimated credit losses at 12/31 110,000\nWhat amount should be reported for accounts receivable,\nbefore allowances for sales returns and credit losses, at\nDecember 31?","options":["$1,200,000","$1,125,000","$1,085,000","$925,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The ending balance in accounts receivable consists of the\n$650,000 beginning balance, plus $2,700,000 of credit sales,\nminus $2,150,000 of collections, $40,000 of accounts written\noff, and $75,000 of sales returns. The ending balance is\n$1,085,000 ($650,000 + 2,700,000 – 2,150,000 – 40,000 –\n75,000). The $110,000 of estimated uncollectible receivables\nand the $50,000 of estimated sales returns are not relevant\nbecause they affect the allowance accounts but not gross\naccounts receivable.","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q224","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":7,"question":"7) An entity had the following sales and accounts receivable\nbalances, prior to any adjustments at year end:\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable\nCredit sales $10,000,000\nAccounts receivable 3,000,000\nAllowance for credit losses 50,000\nThe entity uses 3% of accounts receivable to determine its\nallowance for credit losses at year end. By what amount\nshould the entity adjust its allowance for credit losses at year\nend?","options":["$0","$40,000","$90,000","$140,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The entity uses the percentage of accounts receivable method\nto estimate the allowance. The year-end balance should be\n$90,000 ($3,000,000 A/R × 3%). Hence, the year-end\nadjustment is $40,000 ($90,000 – $50,000) unadjusted\nbalance.","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q225","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":8,"question":"8) When the allowance method of recognizing credit losses is\nused, the entry to record the write-off of a specific account\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable","options":["Decreases both accounts receivable and the allowance for\ncredit losses.","Decreases accounts receivable and increases the allowance\nfor credit losses.","Increases the allowance for credit losses and decreases net\nincome.","Decreases both accounts receivable and net income."],"answer":"A","explanation":"When an account receivable is written off, both accounts\nreceivable and the allowance for credit losses are decreased.\nIf an account previously written off is collected, the account\nmust be reinstated by increasing both accounts receivable and\nthe allowance. The account receivable is then decreased by\nthe amount of cash collected.","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q226","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":9,"question":"9) On March 31, there was an unadjusted credit balance of\n$1,000 in the allowance for credit losses. An analysis of trade\naccounts receivable at that date revealed the following:\nAge Amount\nEstimated\nUncollectible\n0-30 days $60,000 5%\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable\n31-60 days 4,000 10%\nOver 60 days 2,000 70%\nWhat amount should be reported as allowance for credit\nlosses in the March 31 balance sheet?","options":["$4,800","$4,000","$3,800","$3,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The aging schedule determines the balance in the allowance\nfor credit losses. Of the accounts that are no more than 30\ndays old, the amount uncollectible is $3,000 ($60,000 × 5%).\nAccounts that are 31-60 days old and over 60 days old have\nestimated uncollectible balances of $400 ($4,000 × 10%) and\n$1,400 ($2,000 × 70%), respectively. Hence, the amount\nrecorded in the allowance for credit losses is $4,800 ($3,000 +\n$400 + $1,400). The $1,000 balance already in the account is\ndisregarded because the aging schedule determines the\nbalance that should be in the account.","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q227","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":10,"question":"10) Under the allowance method of recognizing credit losses, the\nentry to write-off an uncollectible account\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable","options":["Increases the allowance for credit losses.","Has no effect on the allowance for credit losses.","Has no effect on net income.","Decreases net income."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The entry to record credit loss expense under the allowance\nmethod is to record credit loss expense and increase the\nallowance account. When a specific account is then written\noff, both the allowance and accounts receivable are reduced.\nNet income is affected when credit loss expense is\nrecognized, not at the time of the write-off. Because accounts\nreceivable and the allowance account are decreased by the\nsame amount, a write-off of an account also has no effect on\nthe net amount of accounts receivable.","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q228","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":11,"question":"11) An entity’s allowance for credit loss was $100,000 at the end\nof Year 2 and $90,000 at the end of Year 1. For the year\nended December 31, Year 2, the entity reported credit loss\nexpense of $16,000 in its income statement. What amount did\nthe entity record in Year 2 to write off actual credit losses?","options":["$6,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable","$10,000","$16,000","$26,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"When credit losses are written off, an entry is made to\ndecrease both the allowance and accounts receivable. The\nbeginning balance in the allowance account is $90,000, the\nending balance is $100,000, and the credit loss expense is\n$16,000. Because write-offs equal the beginning balance, plus\nthe credit loss expense, minus the ending balance, $6,000 of\naccounts must have been written off.","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q229","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":12,"question":"12) William Co. determined that the net realizable value (NRV) of\nits accounts receivable at December 31, based on an aging of\nthe receivables, was $650,000. Additional information is as\nfollows:\nAllowance for credit losses -- 1/1 $ 60,000\nCredit losses on accounts written off during the year 36,000\nCredit losses on accounts recovered during the year 4,000\nAccounts receivable at 12/31 700,000\nWhat is William’s credit loss expense for the year?\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable","options":["$10,000","$22,000","$30,000","$42,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The allowance for credit losses before year-end adjustment is\n$28,000 ($60,000 beginning balance – $36,000 write-offs +\n$4,000 recovered). The balance should be $50,000 ($700,000\nyear-end A/R – $650,000 NRV based on aging). Thus, the\nallowance account and credit loss expense should be adjusted\n$22,000 ($50,000 desired balance – $28,000), which is the\nbalance of the credit loss expense reported on the income\nstatement.","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q230","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":13,"question":"13) Ward Co. estimates its credit loss expense to be 2% of credit\nsales. Ward’s credit sales for the current year were $1 million.\nDuring the year, Ward wrote off $18,000 of credit losses.\nWard’s allowance for credit losses had a $15,000 balance on\nJanuary 1. In its December 31 income statement, what amount\nshould Ward report as credit loss expense?","options":["$23,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable","$20,000","$18,000","$17,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"When credit loss expense is estimated on the basis of net\ncredit sales, credit loss expense is directly associated with a\nrevenue of the period (net credit sales). Thus, credit loss\nexpense is $20,000 ($1,000,000 credit sales × 2%).","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q231","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":14,"question":"14) The following information pertains to Eire Co.’s accounts\nreceivable at December 31, Year 2:\nDays Estimated\nOutstanding Amount % Uncollectible\n0 - 60 $240,000 1%\n61 - 120 180,000 2%\nOver 120 200,000 6%\n$620,000\nDuring Year 2, Eire wrote off $14,000 in receivables and\nrecovered $8,000 that was written off in prior years. Its\nDecember 31, Year 1, allowance for credit losses was\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable\n$44,000. Under the aging method, what amount of allowance\nfor credit losses should Eire report at December 31, Year 2?","options":["$18,000","$20,000","$26,000","$38,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The aging schedule determines the allowance for credit losses\nbased on year-end accounts receivable, their age, and their\nestimated collectibility. This year-end amount is $18,000\n[($240,000 × 1%) + ($180,000 × 2%) + ($200,000 × 6%)].","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q232","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":15,"question":"15) The following account balances were abstracted from Pika\nCo.’s records at December 31:\nAccounts receivable $2,000,000\nAllowance for credit losses 16,000\nNet credit sales $6,000,000\nPika estimates that 3% of the gross accounts receivable will\nbecome uncollectible. After adjustment at December 31, the\nallowance for credit losses should have a balance of","options":["$180,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable","$164,000","$44,000","$60,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The allowance for credit losses at year end should have a\nbalance of $60,000. This amount is equal to the $2,000,000 of\naccounts receivable multiplied by the 3% that is estimated to\nbecome uncollectible.","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q233","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":6,"question":"6) An analysis and aging of Hom Company’s accounts receivable\nat December 31 disclosed the following:\nAccounts receivable $850,000\nAllowance for credit losses per books 50,000\nAmounts deemed uncollectible 64,000\nThe net realizable value (NRV) of the accounts receivable at\nDecember 31 should be","options":["$836,000","$800,000","$786,000","$736,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The NRV of accounts receivable is equal to the $850,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable\ngross accounts receivable minus the $64,000 estimate of\ncredit losses. The $50,000 balance in the allowance is not\nused because it is an unadjusted balance.","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q234","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":17,"question":"17) Rue Co.’s allowance for credit losses had a balance of\n$12,000 at December 31, Year 1. During Year 2, Rue wrote off\nuncollectible accounts of $48,000. The aging of accounts\nreceivable indicated that a $50,000 allowance for credit losses\nwas required at December 31, Year 2. What amount of credit\nloss expense should Rue report for Year 2?","options":["$48,000","$50,000","$60,000","$86,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The beginning balance of the allowance for credit losses was\n$12,000. The account was decreased $48,000 when the\nuncollectible accounts were written off. Thus, the entry for\ncredit loss expense must be $86,000 if the ending balance is\n$50,000.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q235","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":18,"question":"18) At December 31, Year 1, Gasp Co.’s allowance for credit\nlosses had a balance of $30,000. During Year 2, Gasp wrote\noff uncollectible accounts of $45,000. At December 31, Year 2,\nan aging of the accounts receivable indicated that $50,000 of\nthe December 31, Year 2, receivables may be uncollectible.\nWhat amount of allowance for credit losses should Gasp report\nin its December 31, Year 2, balance sheet?","options":["$20,000","$25,000","$35,000","$50,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Under the balance sheet approach, the ending balance of the\nallowance for credit losses is a percentage of the ending\nbalance of accounts receivable. The uncollectible accounts\nidentified by the aging schedule at year end reflect the total\nrequired ending balance of the allowance. Since the company\ndetermined that $50,000 of the December 31, Year 2,\nreceivables may be uncollectible, this is the ending balance of\nthe allowance for credit losses.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q236","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":19,"question":"19) At the end of Year 1, Boller Co. had an ending balance in\nallowance for credit losses of $30,000. During Year 2, Boller\nwrote off $40,000 of accounts receivable. At the end of Year 2,\nBoller had $300,000 in accounts receivable and determined\nthat 8% of these would be uncollectible. What amount should\nbe reported as credit loss expense on Boller’s Year 2 income\nstatement?","options":["$64,000","$34,000","$24,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable","$14,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The Year 2 ending balance for allowance for credit losses is\n$24,000 ($300,000 × 8%). The write-off of a particular bad\ndebt has no effect on credit loss expense. It is recognized as a\ndecrease in the balance of allowance for credit losses.\nTherefore, the credit loss expense in Year 2 of $34,000 can be\ncalculated as follows:\n1/1/Year 2 allowance for credit losses $30,000\nAccounts written off (40,000)\nCredit loss expense 34,000\n12/31/Year 2 allowance for credit losses $24,000","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q237","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":20,"question":"20) On April 1, Aloe, Inc., factored $80,000 of its accounts\nreceivable without recourse. The factor retained 10% of the\naccounts receivable as an allowance for sales returns and\ncharged a 5% commission on the gross amount of the factored\nreceivables. What amount of cash did Aloe receive from the\nfactored receivables?","options":["$68,000","$68,400","$72,000","$76,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 1: Accounts Receivable\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals\n2: (107) Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure:\nAssets -- Short-Term Items\n2: (18) Inventory -- Fundamentals\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Factoring is a transfer of receivables to a third party (a factor)\nwho assumes the responsibility of collection. Factoring\ndiscounts receivables on a nonrecourse, notification basis. If a\nsale is without recourse, the transferee (the factor) assumes\nthe risks and rewards of collection. The factor retained 10% of\nthe receivables ($80,000 × 10% = $8,000) as a reserve (an\nallowance for returns) and charged a 5% commission on the\ngross receivables ($80,000 × 5% = $4,000). Accordingly, the\ntransferor received $68,000 ($80,000 – $8,000 – $4,000).","sectionId":"2.1"},{"id":"v1-su2-q238","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":1,"question":"1) A physical inventory count showed an entity had inventory\ncosting $1,000,000 on hand at December 31, Year 1.\nExcluded from this amount were the following:\n Goods costing $82,000, shipped to a customer free on\nboard (FOB) shipping point on December 28, Year 1.\nThey were expected to be received by the customer\non January 4, Year 2.\n Goods costing $122,000, shipped to a customer free\non board (FOB) destination December 30, Year 1.\nThey were expected to be received by the customer\non January 5, Year 2.\nCompute the correct ending inventory to be reported on the\nshipper’s statement of financial position at December 31,\nYear 1.","options":["$1,000,000","$1,082,000","$1,122,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals","$1,204,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goods shipped FOB shipping point should be counted in\nthe buyer’s, not the seller’s, inventory because title and risk of\nloss pass at the time and place of shipment. These goods\nwere properly excluded from ending inventory. The goods\nshipped FOB destination were improperly excluded from the\nseller’s ending inventory. The title and risk of loss did not pass\nuntil the time and place where the goods reached their\ndestination and were duly tendered. Thus, the correct ending\ninventory is $1,122,000 ($1,000,000 beginning balance +\n$122,000 goods shipped FOB destination).","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q239","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":2,"question":"2) The following selected data from statements of financial\nposition on December 31, Year 1, and December 31, Year 2,\nare presented below:\n12/31/Year 1 12/31/Year 2\nInventory $120,000 $140,000\nTrade accounts payable 62,000 49,000\nAdditional information for Year 2:\n1. Cash payments to suppliers of merchandise were\n$180,000.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals\nCost of goods sold in Year 2 was","options":["$147,000","$160,000","$167,000","$180,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Cost of goods sold equals beginning inventory, plus\npurchases, minus ending inventory. To determine cost of\ngoods sold, purchases must be calculated. Purchases equal\n$167,000 ($49,000 ending accounts payable + $180,000\npayments to suppliers – $62,000 beginning accounts\npayable). Thus, cost of goods sold equals $147,000 ($120,000\nbeginning inventory + $167,000 purchases – $140,000 ending\ninventory).","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q240","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":3,"question":"3) An entity with a December 31 year end purchased $2,000 of\ninventory on account. The seller was responsible for delivery\nto the shipping point, with freight of $50 paid at destination by\nthe buyer. The invoice date was December 27, Year 1, and the\ngoods arrived on January 3, Year 2.\nNow assume the terms required the seller to deliver to the\ndestination instead of the shipping point. What is the correct\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals\namount of inventory and freight-in relating to this purchase on\nthe Year 1 financial statements?\nInventory Freight-In","options":["$0 $0","$2,050 $0","$0 $50","$2,000 $50"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Title and risk of loss passed to the buyer at the destination,\nand the seller incurred the expense of delivery to that point.\nThe goods did not arrive until after year end, so they should\nnot be included in Year 1 inventory. Freight-in should also not\nbe recorded until Year 2.","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q241","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":4,"question":"4) If certain goods owned by an entity were not recorded as a\npurchase and were not counted in ending inventory, in error,\nthen","options":["Cost of goods sold for the period will be understated.","Cost of goods sold for the period will be overstated.","Net income for the period will be understated.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals","There will be no effect on cost of goods sold or profit for the\nperiod."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The effects of the errors on cost of goods sold are offsetting.\nPurchases, which increase cost of goods sold, and ending\ninventory, which decreases cost of goods sold, are\nunderstated by the same amount. Neither cost of goods sold\nnor net income is affected.","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q242","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":5,"question":"5) What is the cost of ending inventory given the following\nfactors?\nBeginning inventory $ 5,000\nTotal production costs 60,000\nCost of goods sold 55,000\nDirect labor 40,000","options":["$5,000","$10,000","$45,000","$50,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Beginning inventory, plus purchases (or other inventory\nadditions), minus cost of goods sold, equals ending inventory.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals\nThus, ending inventory equals $10,000 ($5,000 + $60,000 –\n$55,000). Direct labor is included in total production costs.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q243","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":6,"question":"6) The following information applies to the income statement of a\ncompany:\nGross sales $1,000,000\nNet sales 900,000\nFreight-in 10,000\nEnding inventory 200,000\nGross profit margin 40%\nThe company’s cost of goods available for sale is","options":["$550,000","$560,000","$740,000","$800,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The gross profit (gross margin) method calculates ending\ninventory at a given time by subtracting an estimated cost of\ngoods sold from the sum of beginning inventory and\npurchases (or cost of goods manufactured). The estimated\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals\ncost of goods sold equals sales minus the gross profit. The\ngross profit equals sales multiplied by the gross profit\npercentage, an amount ordinarily determined on a historical\nbasis. Given that the gross margin percentage is 40% of net\nsales, cost of goods sold must be 60% of net sales, or\n$540,000 ($900,000 × 60%). Goods available for sale equals\ncost of goods sold plus ending inventory ($540,000 +\n$200,000 = $740,000).","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q244","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":7,"question":"7) A company’s inventory is overstated at December 31 of this\nyear. The result will be","options":["Understated income this year.","Understated retained earnings this year.","Understated retained earnings next year.","Understated income next year."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Cost of goods sold equals beginning finished goods, plus cost\nof goods manufactured for a manufacturer or purchases for a\nretailer, minus ending finished goods. Overstated ending\ninventory therefore results in understated cost of goods sold,\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals\noverstated net income, and overstated retained earnings in\nthe period of the error. When these errors reverse in the\nfollowing period, beginning inventory and cost of goods sold\nwill be overstated, and net income will be understated.\nRetained earnings will be correct.","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q245","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":8,"question":"8) Which one of the following errors will result in the\noverstatement of net income?","options":["Overstatement of beginning inventory.","Overstatement of ending inventory.","Overstatement of goodwill amortization.","Overstatement of allowance for credit losses.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Cost of goods sold equals beginning finished goods, plus cost\nof goods manufactured for a manufacturer or purchases for a\nretailer, minus ending finished goods. Overstated ending\ninventory therefore results in understated cost of goods sold,\noverstated net income, and overstated retained earnings in\nthe period of the error.","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q246","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":9,"question":"9) If ending inventory is underestimated due to an error in the\nphysical count of items on hand, cost of goods sold for the\nperiod will be <List A> and net earnings will be <List B>.\nList A List B","options":["UnderestimatedUnderestimated","Underestimated Overestimated","Overestimated Underestimated","Overestimated Overestimated"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Cost of goods sold equals beginning inventory, plus\npurchases, minus ending inventory. If the ending inventory is\nunderestimated, the cost of goods sold will be overestimated.\nIf cost of goods sold is overestimated, net earnings will be\nunderestimated.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q247","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":10,"question":"10) Heidelberg Co.’s beginning inventory at January 1 was\nunderstated by $52,000, and its ending inventory was\noverstated by $104,000. As a result, Heidelberg’s cost of\ngoods sold for the year was","options":["Understated by $52,000.","Overstated by $52,000.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals","Understated by $156,000.","Overstated by $156,000."],"answer":"C","explanation":"When beginning inventory is understated, cost of goods sold\nwill be understated. When ending inventory is overstated, cost\nof goods sold will be understated. Thus, Heidelberg Co.’s\ninventory is understated by $156,000 ($52,000 + $104,000).","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q248","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":11,"question":"11) The following inventory errors have been discovered for\nLithuania Corporation:\n The Year 1 year-end inventory was overstated by\n$23,000.\n The Year 2 year-end inventory was understated by\n$61,000.\n The Year 3 year-end inventory was understated by\n$17,000.\nThe reported income before taxes for Lithuania was\nYear Income before Taxes\nYear 1 $138,000\nYear 2 254,000\nYear 3 168,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals\nReported income before taxes for Year 1, Year 2, and Year 3,\nrespectively, should have been","options":["$161,000, $170,000, and $212,000.","$115,000, $338,000, and $124,000.","$161,000, $338,000, and $90,000.","$115,000, $338,000, and $212,000."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Cost of sales equals beginning inventory, plus purchases or\ncost of goods manufactured, minus ending inventory. Hence,\nover (under) statement of inventory affects cost of sales and\nincome. The Year 1 pretax income was affected by the\n$23,000 Year 1 overstatement of year-end inventory. This\nerror understated Year 1 cost of sales and overstated pretax\nincome. The corrected income is $115,000 ($138,000 –\n$23,000). The same $23,000 error caused Year 2 income to\nbe understated by overstating beginning inventory. In addition,\nthe $61,000 understatement of Year 2 year-end inventory also\ncaused Year 2 income to be understated. Thus, the corrected\nYear 2 pretax income is $338,000 ($254,000 + $23,000 +\n$61,000). The $61,000 understatement at the end of Year 2\ncaused Year 3 income to be overstated by understating\nbeginning inventory. Income for Year 3 is understated by the\n$17,000 of year-end inventory understatement. Accordingly,\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals\nthe corrected income is $124,000 ($168,000 – $61,000 +\n$17,000).","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q249","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":12,"question":"12) On December 28, Kerr Manufacturing Co. purchased goods\ncosting $50,000. The terms were FOB destination. Some of\nthe costs incurred in connection with the sale and delivery of\nthe goods were as follows:\nPackaging for shipment $1,000\nShipping 1,500\nSpecial handling charges 2,000\nThese goods were received on December 31. In Kerr’s\nDecember 31 balance sheet, what amount of cost for these\ngoods should be included in inventory?","options":["$54,500","$53,500","$52,000","$50,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"FOB destination means that title passes upon delivery at the\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals\ndestination, the seller bears the risk of loss during transit, and\nthe seller is responsible for the expense of delivering the\ngoods to the designated point. Consequently, the packaging,\nshipping, and handling costs are not included in the buyer’s\ninventory. The amount that Kerr should include is therefore the\npurchase price of $50,000.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q250","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":13,"question":"13) A material overstatement in ending inventory was discovered\nafter the year-end financial statements of a company were\nissued to the public. What effect did this error have on the\nyear-end financial statements?\nCurrent assets Gross profit","options":["Understated Overstated","Overstated Overstated","Understated Understated","Overstated Understated"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Inventory is a current asset. Thus, overstating the ending\nbalance overstates current assets. Gross profit equals net\nsales minus cost of goods sold. Cost of goods sold is\ndecreased by ending inventory. Overstating the ending\ninventory understates cost of goods sold and overstates gross\nprofit.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q251","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":14,"question":"14) A firm’s ending inventory balance was overstated by $1,000.\nWhich of the following statements is correct according to a\nperiodic inventory system?","options":["The retained earnings were overstated by $1,000.","The cost of goods sold was overstated by $1,000.","The cost of goods available for sale was overstated by $1,000.","The gross margin was understated by $1,000."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Cost of goods sold (COGS) equals beginning inventory, plus\npurchases during the period, minus ending inventory. Thus, a\n$1,000 overstatement of the ending inventory results in a\n$1,000 understatement of cost of goods sold. The $1,000\nunderstatement of COGS results in a $1,000 overstatement of\ngross profit, and $1,000 overstatement of retained earnings.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q252","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":15,"question":"15) A company uses a periodic inventory system and has its cost\nof ending inventory understated by $4,000. Which of the\nfollowing describes the effects of this error on the company’s\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals\ncurrent-year’s cost of goods sold and net income,\nrespectively?\nCost of goods sold Net income","options":["Understated Understated","Understated Overstated","Overstated Understated","Overstated Overstated"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Cost of goods sold equals beginning inventory, plus cost of\ngoods manufactured for a manufacturer or purchased by a\nretailer, minus ending inventory. An understated ending\ninventory will result in an overstated cost of goods sold and an\nunderstated net income.","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q253","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":16,"question":"16) Herc Co.’s inventory at December 31, Year 1, was $1.5 million\nbased on a physical count priced at cost, and before any\nnecessary adjustment for the following:\n Merchandise costing $90,000 was shipped FOB\nshipping point from a vendor on December 30, Year 1,\nand was received and recorded on January 5, Year 2.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals\n Goods in the shipping area were excluded from\ninventory although shipment was not made until\nJanuary 4, Year 2. The goods, billed to the customer\nFOB shipping point on December 30, Year 1, had a\ncost of $120,000.\nWhat amount should Herc report as inventory in its\nDecember 31, Year 1, balance sheet?","options":["$1,500,000","$1,590,000","$1,620,000","$1,710,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The inventory balance prior to adjustments was $1.5 million.\nThe merchandise shipped FOB shipping point to Herc should\nbe included because title passed when the goods were\nshipped. The goods in the shipping area should be included\nbecause title did not pass until the goods were shipped in\nYear 2. Thus, inventory reported at December 31, Year 1,\nshould be $1,710,000 ($1,500,000 + $90,000 + $120,000).","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q254","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":17,"question":"17) What is the appropriate treatment for goods held on\nconsignment?\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals","options":["The goods should be included in ending inventory of the\nconsignor.","The goods should be included in ending inventory of the\nconsignee.","The goods should be included in cost of goods sold of the\nconsignee only when sold.","The goods should be included in cost of goods sold of the\nconsignor when transferred to the consignee."],"answer":"A","explanation":"A consignment sale is an arrangement between the owner of\ngoods and a sales agent. Consigned goods are not sold but\nrather transferred to the agent (consignee) for possible sale.\nThe consignor records sales only when the goods are sold to\nthird parties by the consignee. Thus, unsold consigned goods\nare included in inventory at cost.","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q255","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":18,"question":"18) The Super Toy Store inventory records at December 31\nrevealed the following:\nInventory on hand, December 31 $350,000\nMerchandise purchased F.O.B. shipping point,\nshipped by vendor on December 31, expected\ndelivery date–January 4 118,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals\nMerchandise shipped to customers on\nDecember 28 F.O.B. destination, expected delivery\ndate–January 3 75,000\nGoods held on consignment by Super Toy Store,\nnot included in inventory on hand 38,000\nWhat was Super Toy Store’s ending inventory at December\n31?","options":["$350,000","$393,000","$468,000","$543,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Items should be included in inventory when Super Toy Store\nhas control over the items. For the goods purchased under\nFOB shipping point, control of the goods passes to Super Toy\nStore when the seller makes a proper tender of delivery of the\ngoods to the carrier (i.e., on December 31). For the goods sold\nunder FOB destination, Super Toy Store has control until the\ngoods arrive at the destination (i.e., on January 3). Goods held\non consignment are never recorded as inventory by the\nconsignee (i.e., Super Toy Store). Thus, Super Toy Store’s\nending inventory on December 31 includes the inventory on\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 2: Inventory -- Fundamentals\nhand, the purchased merchandise, and the merchandise\nshipped but not yet delivered, for a total of $543,000\n($350,000 + $118,000 + $75,000).\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\n2: (107) Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure:\nAssets -- Short-Term Items\n3: (37) Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q256","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":1,"question":"1) The inventory method yielding the same inventory\nmeasurement and cost of goods sold whether a perpetual or\nperiodic system is used is","options":["Average cost.","First-in, first-out.","Last-in, first-out.","Either first-in, first-out or last-in, first-out."],"answer":"B","explanation":"A perpetual inventory system will result in the same dollar\namount of ending inventory as a periodic inventory system\nassuming a FIFO cost flow. Under both perpetual and periodic\nsystems, the same units are deemed to be in ending\ninventory.","sectionId":"2.4"},{"id":"v1-su2-q257","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":2,"question":"2) An entity started in Year 1 with 200 scented candles on hand\nat a cost of $3.50 each. These candles sell for $7.00 each.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\nThe following schedule represents the purchases and sales of\ncandles during Year 1:\nTransaction\nNumber\nQuantity\nPurchased\nUnit\nCost\nQuantity\nSold\n1 --- --- 150\n2 250 $3.30 ---\n3 --- --- 100\n4 200 3.10 ---\n5 --- --- 200\n6 350 3.00 ---\n7 --- --- 300\nIf the entity uses periodic FIFO inventory pricing, the gross\nprofit for Year 1 would be","options":["$2,755","$2,805","$2,854","$2,920"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The FIFO method assumes that the first goods purchased are\nthe first goods sold and that ending inventory consists of the\nlatest purchases. Moreover, whether the inventory system is\nperiodic or perpetual does not affect FIFO measurement. The\ncost of goods sold is $2,445 {beginning inventory (200 units ×\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\n$3.50) + purchases [(250 units × $3.30) + (200 units × $3.10)\n+ (350 units × $3.00)] – ending inventory (250 units × $3.00)}.\nThus, the gross profit for Year 1 using FIFO is $2,805 [sales\n(750 units × $7.00) – cost of goods sold of $2,445].","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q258","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":3,"question":"3) The cost of materials has risen steadily over the year. Which of\nthe following methods of estimating the ending balance of the\nmaterials inventory account will result in the highest profit,\nassuming all other variables remain constant?","options":["Last-in, first-out (LIFO).","First-in, first-out (FIFO).","Weighted average.","Specific identification."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Profit will be higher when cost of goods sold is lower, other\nfactors held constant. Cost of goods sold equals beginning\ninventory, plus purchases, minus ending inventory.\nAccordingly, cost of goods sold will be lowest when the ending\ninventory is highest. In an inflationary environment, ending\ninventory is highest under FIFO because the older, less\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\nexpensive items are deemed to have been sold, leaving the\nmore expensive items in the ending inventory.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q259","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":4,"question":"4) When a right of return exists, an entity may recognize revenue\nfrom a sale of goods at the time of sale only if","options":["The amount of future returns can be reliably estimated.","The seller retains the risks and rewards of ownership.","The buyer resells the goods.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods","The seller believes returns will not be material.\nFact Pattern: Illustrated below is a perpetual inventory card for the\ncurrent year.\nDate Units Purchased Units Sold Units Balance\nJanuary 1 0\nJanuary 12 1,000 @ $2.00 1,000\nMarch 15 300 700\nMay 5 500 @ $2.20 1,200\nJuly 8 500 700\nNovember 24 1,000 @ $1.65 1,700\nAdditional information:\n The entity had no opening inventory.\n The items sold on March 15 were purchased on January 12.\n The items sold on July 8 were purchased on May 5."],"answer":"A","explanation":"One condition for recognition of revenue from the sale of\ngoods is the transfer of the significant risks and rewards of\nownership. Retention of significant risk may occur when, for\nexample, the buyer may rescind the purchase for a reason\nstipulated in the contract, and the buyer is uncertain about the\nprobability of return. However, if the entity can reliably\nestimate future returns and recognizes a liability for returns\nbased on experience and other pertinent information, revenue\nmay be recognized at the time of sale if the other conditions\nfor revenue recognition also are met.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q260","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":5,"question":"5) The ending inventory balance under the first-in, first-out (FIFO)\nmethod of inventory valuation is","options":["$3,050","$3,150\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods","$3,230","$3,430"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Under the FIFO method, the 1,700 units of ending inventory\nare valued at the most recent prices. Ending inventory is\nassumed to include 1,000 units purchased November 24, 500\nunits purchased May 5, and 200 units purchased January 12.\nHence, the ending inventory is $3,150 [(1,000 × $1.65) + (500\n× $2.20) + (200 × $2.00)].","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q261","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":6,"question":"6) The cost of goods sold under the specific identification method\nof inventory valuation is","options":["$1,320","$1,520","$1,600","$1,700"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Of the 800 units sold during the period, the 300 units sold on\nMarch 15 were purchased on January 12 at a cost of $2.00\nper unit. The remaining 500 units were purchased on May 5 at\na cost of $2.20 per unit. The cost of goods sold under the\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\nspecific identification method is therefore $1,700 [(300 units ×\n$2.00) + (500 units × $2.20)].","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q262","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":7,"question":"7) A merchandising company had the following inventory related\ntransactions in its first year of operations:\nDate\nPurchases\nin Units\nSales\nin Units\nBalance\nin Units\nJan. 1 10,000 @ $5 10,000\nMarch 1 6,000 @ $6 16,000\nMay 1 3,000 13,000\nJuly 1 8,000 @ $6.25 21,000\nSept. 1 12,000 9,000\nNov. 1 5,000 @ $7 14,000\nDec. 1 2,000 12,000\nIf the company uses the first-in-first-out (FIFO) method of\ninventory valuation, its ending inventory balance (rounded) will\nbe","options":["$62,000","$70,759","$78,750\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods","$84,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The first-in-first-out (FIFO) method treats the oldest units as\nbeing sold first and the newest units remain in inventory.\nBecause the company has 12,000 units remaining, ending\ninventory equals $78,750 [(5,000 × $7) + (7,000 × $6.25)].","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q263","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":8,"question":"8) Which of the following changes in accounting policies resulting\nfrom a significant change in the expected pattern of economic\nbenefit will increase profit?","options":["A change from FIFO to LIFO inventory valuation when costs\nare rising.","A change from FIFO to weighted-average inventory valuation\nwhen costs are falling.","A change from accelerated to straight-line depreciation in the\nlater years of the depreciable lives of the assets.","A change from straight-line to accelerated depreciation in the\nearly years of the depreciable lives of the assets."],"answer":"B","explanation":"In a period of falling costs, FIFO results in higher cost of\ngoods sold than the weighted-average method. FIFO includes\nthe higher, earlier costs in cost of goods sold, and the\nweighted-average method averages the later, lower costs with\nthe higher, earlier costs. Thus, a change from FIFO to\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\nweighted-average costing reduces cost of goods sold and\nincreases reported profit.","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q264","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":9,"question":"9) On January 1, a company has no opening inventory balance.\nThe following purchases are made during the year:\nUnits\nPurchased\nUnit\nCost\nJanuary 1 5,000 $10.00\nApril 1 5,000 9.00\nJuly 1 5,000 8.00\nOctober 1 5,000 7.50\nThere are 10,000 units in inventory on December 31.\nIf the company uses the last-in, first-out (LIFO) method of\ninventory valuation, cost of goods sold for the year will be:","options":["$77,500","$86,250","$87,500","$95,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A total of 20,000 units was available for sale (0 beginning\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\ninventory + 20,000 purchased during year). Because 10,000\nremain in ending inventory, 10,000 were sold\n(20,000 available – 10,000 remaining). Under the LIFO\nmethod, the units sold were those purchased in July and\nOctober. Cost of goods sold for the year thus equaled $77,500\n[(5,000 units × $8.00 July) + (5,000 units × $7.50 October)].","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q265","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":10,"question":"10) The advantage of the last-in, first-out inventory method is\nbased on the assumption that","options":["The most recently incurred costs should be allocated to the\ncost of goods sold.","Costs should be charged to revenue in the order in which they\nare incurred.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods","Costs should be charged to cost of goods sold at average\ncost.","Current costs should be based on representative or normal\nconditions of efficiency and volume of operations."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Under the LIFO method, the most recent costs of acquiring or\nproducing inventory are expensed as part of cost of goods\nsold. Given inflation, this method results in the highest cost of\ngoods.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q266","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":11,"question":"11) Which inventory cost flow method is prohibited according to\nIFRS?","options":["First-in, first-out (FIFO) method.","Specific identification method.","Weighted average cost method.","Last-in, first-out (LIFO) method."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The last-in, first-out (LIFO) method is prohibited by IFRS. This\nmethod is based on the assumption that the newest items are\nsold first. Its effect is to include current prices in cost of goods\nsold. But the LIFO assumption ordinarily does not match\nactual inventory use.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q267","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":12,"question":"12) In a period of rising prices, which one of the following inventory\nmethods usually provides the best matching of expenses\nagainst revenues?\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods","options":["Weighted average.","First-in, first-out.","Last-in, first-out.","Specific identification."],"answer":"C","explanation":"A significant advantage of the LIFO method is its matching of\ncurrent revenues with the most recent product costs. When\nprices are rising (which is most of the time), the most recent\ncosts are the highest costs, resulting in higher cost of goods\nsold and lower net income. The lower net income means lower\ntaxes.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q268","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":13,"question":"13) In periods of rising costs, which one of the following inventory\ncost flow assumptions will result in higher cost of sales?","options":["First-in, first-out.","Last-in, first-out.","Weighted average.","Moving average."],"answer":"B","explanation":"A significant advantage of the LIFO method is its matching of\ncurrent revenues with the most recent product costs. When\nprices are rising (which is most of the time), the most recent\ncosts are the highest costs, resulting in higher cost of goods\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\nsold and lower net income. The lower net income means lower\ntaxes.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q269","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":14,"question":"14) An entity has 8,000 units in inventory on January 1, valued at\n$10 per unit. During the year, the entity sold 25,000 units and\npurchased inventory as follows:\nDate\nQuantity\nPurchased Unit Price\nApril 1 15,000 units $ 8\nJuly 1 10,000 units 9\nOctober 1 12,500 units 10\nIf the entity uses the weighted-average method of inventory\nvaluation, cost of goods sold for the period will be","options":["$186,978","$197,000","$228,023","$235,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Under the weighted-average method, the weighted-average\ncost per unit is multiplied by the number of units sold to\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\ndetermine the cost of goods sold for the period. The total units\navailable for sale equaled 45,500 (8,000 + 15,000 + 10,000 +\n12,500). The total cost of all units available for sale was\n$415,000 [(8,000 × $10) + (15,000 × $8) + (10,000 × $9) +\n(12,500 × $10)]. Thus, the weighted-average cost per unit of\ninventory was $9.1209 ($415,000 ÷ 45,500), and cost of goods\nsold was $228,023 (25,000 × $9.1209).","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q270","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":15,"question":"15) Which inventory pricing method generally approximates\ncurrent cost for each of the following?\nEnding\nInventory\nCost of\nGoods Sold","options":["FIFO FIFO","LIFO FIFO","FIFO LIFO","LIFO LIFO"],"answer":"C","explanation":"FIFO assigns the most recent acquisition costs to ending\ninventory and the earliest acquisition costs to cost of goods\nsold. LIFO assigns the earliest acquisition costs to ending\ninventory (it is permitted by U.S. GAAP but not by IFRS).\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\nThus, FIFO approximates current cost for ending inventory,\nand LIFO approximates current cost of goods sold.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q271","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":16,"question":"16) Which one of the following actions would result in a decrease\nin income?","options":["Liquidating last-in, first-out layers of inventory when prices\nhave been increasing.","Changing from first-in, first-out to last-in, first-out inventory\nmethod when prices are decreasing.","Accelerating purchases at the end of the year when using last-\nin, first-out inventory method in times of rising prices.","Changing the number of last-in, first-out pools."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Under the LIFO method, the most recent costs of acquiring or\nproducing inventory are expensed as part of cost of goods\nsold. In a time of rising prices, charging newer, higher-priced\ngoods against current revenues decreases income.","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q272","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":17,"question":"17) A retailer sells antique replica trunks to customers all over the\nworld. The retailer’s inventory records show the following:\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\nQuantity\n(units)\nCost\n(each)\nBeginning inventory 200 $1,055\nPurchases:\nJune 3 170 1,062\nSeptember 18 190 1,070\nDecember 10 160 1,076\nThe retailer sells 470 units this year. Management is\nresearching whether the company should use last in, first out\n(LIFO) or first in, first out (FIFO). If the retailer’s management\nwants to lower the company’s income taxes, which inventory\ncost flow assumption should it select?","options":["FIFO, because the cost of goods sold will be $9,870 higher\nthan LIFO.","FIFO, because the operating income will be $840 lower than\nLIFO.","LIFO, because the operating income will be $4,360 lower than\nFIFO.","LIFO, because the cost of goods sold will be $5,250 higher\nthan FIFO."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The FIFO method assumes that the first goods purchased are\nthe first goods sold and that ending inventory consists of the\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\nlatest purchases. Thus, the cost of goods sold under FIFO is\n$498,540 [(200 units × $1,055) + (170 units × $1,062) +\n(100 units × $1,070)]. Under the LIFO method, the most recent\ncosts of acquiring or producing inventory are expensed as part\nof cost of goods sold. Thus, the cost of goods sold under LIFO\nis $502,900 [(160 units × $1,076) + (190 units × $1,070) +\n(120 units × $1,062)]. In order to lower tax income, the\ncompany should use the LIFO method because the cost of\ngoods sold under LIFO is more than the cost of goods sold\nunder FIFO. Therefore, the operating income under LIFO is\n$4,360 ($502,900 – $498,540) lower than the operating\nincome under FIFO.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q273","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":18,"question":"18) Flex Co. uses a periodic inventory system. The following are\ninventory transactions for the month of January:\n1/1 Beginning inventory 10,000 units at $3\n1/5 Purchase 5,000 units at $4\n1/15 Purchase 5,000 units at $5\n1/20 Sales at $10 per unit 10,000 units\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\nFlex uses the average pricing method to determine the value\nof its inventory. What amount should Flex report as cost of\ngoods sold on its income statement for the month of January?","options":["$30,000","$37,500","$40,000","$100,000\nFact Pattern: During January, Metro Co., which maintains a\nperpetual inventory system, recorded the following information\npertaining to its inventory:\nUnits\nUnit\nCost\nTotal\nCost\nUnits On\nHand\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\nBalance\non 1/1\n1,000 $1 $1,000 1,000\nPurchase\nd on 1/7\n600 3 1,800 1,600\nSold on\n1/20\n900 700\nPurchase\nd on 1/25\n400 5 2,000 1,100"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The total cost of beginning inventory and purchases is\n$75,000 ($30,000 + $20,000 + $25,000), and the total number\nunits of beginning inventory and purchases is 20,000. The\naverage price of the beginning inventory and purchases is\n$3.75 ($75,000 cost ÷ 20,000 units). The total cost of goods\nsold equals $37,500 (10,000 units sold × $3.75).","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q274","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":19,"question":"19) Under the moving-average method, what amount should Metro\nreport as inventory at January 31?","options":["$2,640","$3,225","$3,300","$3,900"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The moving-average system is only applicable to perpetual\ninventories. It requires that a new weighted average be\ncomputed after every purchase. This moving average is based\non remaining inventory held and the new inventory purchased.\nBased on the calculations below, the moving-average cost per\nunit for the 1/20 sale is $1.75, and the cost of goods sold\n(COGS) for January is $1,575 (900 units sold × $1.75). Thus,\nending inventory is $3,225 ($1,000 beginning balance +\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\n$1,800 purchase on 1/7 – $1,575 COGS on 1/20 + $2,000\npurchase on 1/25).\nUnits\nMoving-Average\nCost/Unit Total Cost\nBalance 1/1 1,000 $1.00 $1,000\nPurchase 1/7 600 3.00 1,800\n1,600 $1.75 $2,800","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q275","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":20,"question":"20) The weighted average for the year inventory cost flow method\nis applicable to which of the following inventory systems?\nPeriodic Perpetual","options":["Yes Yes","Yes No","No Yes","No No"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The weighted-average method determines an average cost\nonly once (at the end of the period) and is therefore applicable\nonly to a periodic system. In contrast, the moving-average\nmethod requires determination of a new weighted-average\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\ncost after each purchase and thus applies only to a perpetual\nsystem.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q276","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":21,"question":"21) On December 1, a company had 1,000 units in inventory\nvalued at $787,500. On December 12, the company\npurchased 2,000 units for $1,562,400. Sales of 2,400 units\nwere made on December 23, and on December 30, the\ncompany purchased another 2,000 units for $1,537,200. If the\ncompany uses a periodic system and the weighted-average\ninventory valuation method, the company’s December 31\nbalance sheet would report inventory of","options":["$2,025,660","$2,021,292","$2,014,740","$2,007,180"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Using a periodic inventory system, the ending value is not\naffected by when the sales and purchases of units occurred.\nThe company had 5,000 units available during the period\n(1,000 on December 1, 2,000 on December 12, and 2,000 on\nDecember 30). The total cost of these 5,000 units was\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\n$3,887,100 ($787,500 + $1,562,400 + 1,537,200), or $777.42\nper unit ($3,887,100 ÷ 5,000 units). Since 2,400 units were\nsold, the ending inventory would be $2,021,292 (2,600 units ×\n$777.42 per unit).","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q277","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":22,"question":"22) During inflationary periods, last-in, first-out inventory\naccounting will generally have a","options":["Lower cost of goods sold and lower inventory value.","Lower cost of goods sold and higher inventory value.","Higher cost of goods sold and higher inventory value.","Higher cost of goods sold and lower inventory value."],"answer":"D","explanation":"During periods of inflation, the cost of inventory\npurchased/produced throughout the period increases.\nBecause last-in, first-out (LIFO) inventory accounting uses the\nmost recent inventory costs for cost of goods sold, the cost of\ngoods sold reported is generally higher than cost of goods\nsold under a different inventory accounting method. Because\nthe higher inventory costs are assigned to cost of goods sold,\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\nthe cheaper inventory costs from the beginning of the period\nare assigned to ending inventory. This results in a lower\nending inventory valuation.\nFact Pattern: Toulouse Co. began the month of November with\n150 baubles on hand at a cost of $4.00 each. These baubles sell for\n$7.00 each. The following schedule presents the sales and purchases\nof this item during the month of November.\nPurchases\nDate of\nTransaction\nQuantity\nReceived\nUnit\nCost\nUnits\nSold\nNovember 5 100\nNovember 7 200 $4.20\nNovember 9 150\nNovember 11 200 4.40\nNovember 17 220\nNovember 22 250 4.80\nNovember 29 100\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q278","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":23,"question":"23) If Toulouse uses perpetual LIFO inventory pricing, the\ninventory at November 30 will be","options":["$936","$1,012","$1,046","$1,076"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Under perpetual LIFO, the inventory valuation is recalculated\nas follows after every purchase and sale. The 230 units in\nending inventory consist of 150 units at $4.80 each, 30 units at\n$4.20 each, and 50 units from the beginning inventory at\n$4.00 each.\nEnding\nDate Receipts Sales Inventory\n11-1 150 × $4.00 = $600 $ 600.00\n11-5 100 × $4.00 =\n$400\n200.00\n11-7 200 × $4.20 = $840 1,040.00\n11-9 150 × $4.20 =\n$630\n410.00\n11-11 200 × $4.40 = $880 1,290.00\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\n11-17 200 × $4.40 =\n$880\n20 × $4.20 = $84 326.00\n11-22 250 × $4.80 =\n$1,200\n1,526.00\n11-29 100 × $4.80 =\n$480\n1,046.00","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q279","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":24,"question":"24) If Toulouse uses FIFO inventory pricing, the inventory on\nNovember 30 will be","options":["$936","$1,012","$1,046","$1,104"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Under FIFO, the ending inventory consists of the most recent\ninventory purchased. The beginning inventory included 150\nunits and purchases totaled 650 units, a total of 800 units.\nSales equaled 570 units (100 + 150 + 220 + 100). Thus,\nending inventory was 230 units (800 – 570). Under FIFO,\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\nthese units are valued at the cost of the most recent 230 units\npurchased, or $4.80. Ending inventory is therefore $1,104\n(230 × $4.80).","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q280","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":25,"question":"25) If Toulouse uses perpetual moving-average inventory pricing,\nthe sale of 220 items on November 17 will be recorded at a\nunit cost of","options":["$4.00","$4.16","$4.20","$4.32"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The beginning inventory consisted of 150 units at $4.00 each.\nFollowing the November 5 sale, the inventory valuation was\n$200 (50 units × $4). The November 7 purchase added\n200 units at $4.20, after which the moving average unit cost\nwas $4.16 {[$200 + (200 units × $4.20)] ÷ (50 units +\n200 units)}. The November 9 sale of 150 units left 100 units at\n$4.16. Adding $416 (100 units × $4.16) to the $880 purchase\non November 11 brought the total inventory to 300 units with a\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\ntotal cost of $1,296, or $4.32 each. Thus, $4.32 was the unit\ncost of items sold on November 17.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q281","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":26,"question":"26) If Toulouse uses weighted-average inventory pricing, the gross\nprofit for November will be","options":["$1,046","$1,482","$1,516\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods","$1,528"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The total goods available for sale is determined as follows:\nBeginning inventory 150 × $4.00 = $ 600.00\nNov. 7 purchase 200 × $4.20 = 840.00\nNov. 11 purchase 200 × $4.40 = 880.00\nNov. 22 purchase 250 × $4.80 = 1,200.00\nTotal available 800 $3,520.00\nThe weighted-average unit cost is $4.40 ($3,520 ÷ 800 units\navailable). The cost of goods sold and total sales are therefore\n$2,508 (570 units sold × $4.40) and $3,990 (570 units × $7),\nrespectively. Consequently, gross profit is $1,482 ($3,990 –\n$2,508).","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q282","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":27,"question":"27) If Toulouse uses periodic LIFO inventory pricing, the cost of\ngoods sold for November will be","options":["$2,416","$2,444","$2,474","$2,584"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goods available for sale are as follows:\nBeginning inventory 150 × $4.00 = $ 600.00\nNov. 7 purchase 200 × $4.20 = 840.00\nNov. 11 purchase 200 × $4.40 = 880.00\nNov. 22 purchase 250 × $4.80 = 1,200.00\nTotal available 800 $3,520.00\nThe ending inventory consists of 230 units. Under periodic\nLIFO, these are costed at the prices paid for the earliest\n230 units purchased, or 150 units at $4.00 and 80 units at\n$4.20, a total of $936. Accordingly, cost of goods sold is\n$2,584 ($3,520 goods available – $936 EI).\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q283","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":28,"question":"28) Sackett Corporation had a beginning inventory of 10,000 units,\nwhich were purchased in the prior year as follows:\nUnits Unit Price\nSeptember 4,000 $2.00\nOctober 4,000 $2.10\nDecember 2,000 $2.30\nIn the current year, Sackett purchases an additional 12,000\nunits (7,000 in June at $2.50 and 5,000 in November at $2.70)\nand sells 16,000 units. Using the FIFO method, what is\nSackett’s ending inventory?","options":["$12,200 (4,000 @ $2.00 and 2,000 @ $2.10)","$13,000 (4,000 @ $2.10 and 2,000 @ $2.30)","$15,600 (6,000 @ $2.60 – average of $2.50 and $2.70)","$16,000 (5,000 @ $2.70 and 1,000 @ $2.50)"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Under FIFO, the first goods purchased are assumed to be the\nfirst sold. Using FIFO, all of the 10,000 units of inventory in\nbeginning inventory were sold and 6,000 (16,000 sold –\n10,000 beginning inventory) of the units purchased in June for\n$2.50 each were sold. This leaves in ending inventory 1,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\nunits purchased in June for $2.50 each and all 5,000 units\npurchased in November for $2.70 each.","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q284","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":29,"question":"29) The LIFO inventory cost flow method may be applied to which\nof the following inventory systems?\nPeriodic Perpetual","options":["No No","No Yes","Yes Yes","Yes No\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods"],"answer":"C","explanation":"In a periodic system, a purchases account is used, and the\nbeginning inventory remains unchanged during the accounting\nperiod. Cost of goods sold is determined at year-end. It is the\ndifference between the goods available for sale (Beginning\ninventory + Purchases) and ending inventory.\nIn a perpetual system, purchases are directly recorded in the\ninventory account. Cost of goods sold is determined as the\ngoods are sold. LIFO may be applied to both a periodic and a\nperpetual system, but the amount of cost of goods sold may\nvary with the system chosen.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q285","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":30,"question":"30) During periods of rising prices, a perpetual inventory system\nwould result in the same dollar amount of ending inventory as\na periodic inventory system under which of the following\ninventory cost flow methods?\nFIFO LIFO","options":["Yes No","Yes Yes\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods","No Yes","No No"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Under a FIFO cost flow, ending inventory and cost of goods\nsold are the same regardless of whether a perpetual or\nperiodic system is used. Under a LIFO cost flow, on the other\nhand, a perpetual inventory system will generate dollar\namounts different from those of a periodic inventory system.\nDuring a time of rising prices, goods sold and ending inventory\nwill consist of units bearing different costs. The periodic\nsystem determines the cost of sales only at year end. The\nperpetual system determines cost of sales as the sale takes\nplace. Thus, the perpetual system assumes that layers of\ninventory may be liquidated during a year even though\ninventory quantities are restored by later purchases. The\nperiodic system does not make this assumption.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q286","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":31,"question":"31) A company had 2,000 units of opening inventory that cost $20\nper unit. On May 1, 2,000 units were purchased at a cost of\n$22 each, and on September 1, another 2,000 units were\npurchased at a cost of $24 each. If 4,000 units were sold\nduring the year, the company will report cost of goods sold of\n<List A> if the <List B> method of inventory valuation is used.\nList A List B","options":["$88,000 LIFO","$92,000 Weighted-average","$84,000 FIFO","$88,000 FIFO\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Under FIFO, the first items purchased are presumed to be the\nfirst sold. Furthermore, under FIFO, perpetual and periodic\nsystems produce the same ending inventory and cost of\ngoods sold. If 6,000 units were available and 4,000 units were\nsold, FIFO cost of goods sold equals $84,000 [(2,000 × $20)\nBI + (2,000 × $22) May 1 purchase].","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q287","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":32,"question":"32) Generally, which inventory costing method most closely\napproximates the current cost for each of the following?\nCost of Goods Sold Ending Inventory","options":["LIFO FIFO","LIFO LIFO","FIFO FIFO","FIFO LIFO\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The LIFO basis assumes that the most recently purchased\nitems are the first to be sold. Thus, LIFO is a better\napproximation of current cost of goods sold than FIFO.\nAssuming that turnover is rapid and material amounts of\ndepreciation are not allocated to inventory, LIFO cost of goods\nsold may be an acceptable alternative to cost of goods sold\nmeasured at current cost. However, the effect of any LIFO\ninventory liquidations (decreases in earlier years’ LIFO layers)\nmust be excluded. Nevertheless, the FIFO basis more closely\napproximates the current cost of ending inventory because it\nassumes the most recent purchases are the last to be sold.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q288","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":33,"question":"33) The Hastings Company began operations on January 1, Year\n1, and uses the FIFO method in costing its raw material\ninventory. Management is contemplating a change to the LIFO\nmethod and is interested in determining what effect such a\nchange will have on net income. Accordingly, the following\ninformation has been developed:\nFinal Inventory Year 1 Year 2\nFIFO $240,000 $270,000\nLIFO 200,000 210,000\nNet income per FIFO $120,000 $170,000\nBased upon the above information, a change to the LIFO\nmethod in Year 2 results in net income for Year 2 of","options":["$110,000","$150,000","$170,000","$230,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A change in accounting principle requires retrospective\napplication. All periods reported must be individually adjusted\nfor the period-specific effects of applying the new principle.\nThe difference in income in the second year is equal to the\n$20,000 difference between the FIFO inventory change and\nthe LIFO inventory change (FIFO: $270,000 – $240,000 =\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\n$30,000 change; LIFO: $210,000 – $200,000 =\n$10,000 change; $30,000 – $10,000 = $20,000 difference).\nThe $170,000 FIFO net income will decrease by $20,000. Net\nLIFO income will therefore be $150,000 ($170,000 – $20,000).","sectionId":"2.2"},{"id":"v1-su2-q289","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":34,"question":"34) Beck Co.’s inventory is as follows:\nBeginning inventory 10 trees at $ 50\nMarch 4 purchased 6 trees at 55\nMarch 12 sold 8 trees at 100\nMarch 20 purchased 9 trees at 60\nMarch 27 sold 7 trees at 105\nMarch 30 purchased 4 trees at 65\nWhat was Beck’s cost of goods sold using the last-in, first-out\n(LIFO) perpetual method?","options":["$910","$850","$808","$775"],"answer":"B","explanation":"LIFO assumes that the newest items of inventory are sold first.\nThus, the items remaining in inventory are recognized as if\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\nthey were the oldest. In a perpetual system, purchases are\ndirectly recorded in inventory. Cost of goods sold is calculated\nwhen a sale occurs and consists of the latest purchases. The\ncost of goods sold on the sale on (1) March 12 is $430 [(6 ×\n$55) + (2 × $50)] and (2) March 27 is $420 (7 × $60). The total\ncost of goods sold is $850 ($430 + $420).","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q290","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":35,"question":"35) Cobb, Inc.’s inventory at May 1 consisted of 200 units at a total\ncost of $1,250. Cobb uses the periodic inventory method.\nPurchases for the month were as follows:\nDate No. of Units Unit cost Total Cost\nMay 4 20 $5.80 $116.00\nMay 17 80 $5.50 $440.00\nCobb sold 10 units on May 14 for $120. What is Cobb’s\nweighted average cost of goods sold for May?","options":["$60.20","$62.10","$62.50","$65.00"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The weighted-average method is used under the periodic\ninventory method. The average cost is determined only at the\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\nend of the period. The weighted-average cost per unit is used\nto determine the ending inventory and the cost of goods sold\nfor the period. It is calculated as follows: [(Cost of beginning\ninventory + Cost of purchases during the period) ÷ (Units in\nbeginning inventory + Units purchased during the period)].\nTherefore, the weighted-average cost per unit for May is $6.02\n[($1,250 + $116 + $440) ÷ (200 + 20 + 80)]. Cobb’s weighted-\naverage cost of goods sold for May is $60.20 ($6.02 per unit ×\n10 units sold).","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q291","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":36,"question":"36) Ultra Co. uses a periodic inventory system. The following are\ninventory transactions for the month of January:\n1/1 Beginning inventory 20,000 units at $13\n1/20 Purchase 30,000 units at $15\n1/23 Purchase 40,000 units at $17\n1/31 Sales at $20 per unit 50,000 units\nUltra uses the LIFO method to determine the value of its\ninventory. What amount should Ultra report as cost of goods\nsold on its income statement for the month of January?\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods","options":["$710,000","$750,000","$830,000","$1,000,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"In a periodic system, cost of goods sold and ending inventory\nare determined only at the end of the period. Under last-in,\nfirst-out (LIFO) in a periodic system, the 50,000 units sold\nconsists of the 40,000 units purchased on 1/23 at $17 per unit\nand the 10,000 units purchased on 1/20 at $15 per unit. The\ncost of goods sold on the company’s income statement for the\nmonth of January is $830,000 [(40,000 × $17) + (10,000 ×\n$15)].","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q292","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":37,"question":"37) Carver Co., a retailer, uses the perpetual inventory method.\nCarver uses the moving-average method to determine the\nvalue of its inventory. The following information relates to\ninventory transactions that took place during the month of\nMarch:\n3/1 Beginning inventory 30,000 units at $10\n3/5 Purchase 10,000 units at $12\n3/10 Sales at $20 per unit 20,000 units\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 3: Inventory -- Cost Flow Methods\n3/20 Purchase 20,000 units at $13\nWhat amount should Carver report as cost of goods sold on its\nincome statement at the end of March?","options":["$200,000","$210,000","$240,000","$260,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\n2: (107) Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure:\nAssets -- Short-Term Items\n4: (32) Measurement of Inventory Subsequent to Initial\nRecognition\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory Subsequent\nto Initial Recognition"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The moving-average method requires the determination of a\nnew weighted-average cost after each purchase and thus is\nused only in a perpetual system. Since the only sale in March\nwas on 3/10, the cost of goods sold is $210,000 (20,000 units\nsold × $10.50).\nUnits\nCost per\nUnit\nInventory Total\nBalance\nBeg. balance\n3/1\n30,000 $10.00 $300,000\nPurchase 3/5 10,000 12.00 120,000\n40,000 $10.50 $420,000","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q293","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":1,"question":"1) In accounting for inventories, generally accepted accounting\nprinciples require departure from the historical cost principle\nwhen the utility of inventory has fallen below cost. Under the\n“lower-of-cost-or-market” rule, the term “market” means","options":["Original cost minus allowance for obsolescence.","Original cost plus normal profit margin.","Replacement cost of the inventory.","Original cost minus cost to dispose."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Market is the replacement cost of the inventory as determined\nin the market in which the entity buys its inventory, not the\nmarket in which it sells to customers. It is limited to a ceiling\namount equal to net realizable value and a floor amount equal\nto net realizable value minus a normal profit margin.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q294","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":2,"question":"2) The following data apply to a unit of inventory:\nSelling price $22\nSelling cost 2\nNormal profit margin 5\nReplacement cost 10\nUsing the lower-of-cost-or-market (LCM) method of measuring\ninventory, what is the market amount for this unit of inventory?","options":["$10.00","$15.00","$17.50","$20.00"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Under the LCM method, market is current replacement cost\nsubject to a maximum (ceiling) equal to net realizable value\nand a minimum (floor) equal to net realizable value minus a\nnormal profit margin. NRV equals selling price minus costs of\ncompletion and disposal. Thus, the maximum market amount\nis the $20 NRV ($22 selling price – $2 selling cost), and the\nminimum is $15 ($20 NRV – $5 normal profit margin).\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\nBecause the minimum exceeds the $10 replacement cost, it is\nthe market amount.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q295","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":3,"question":"3) A distribution company has determined its December 31\ninventory on a LIFO basis at $200,000. Information pertaining\nto that inventory follows:\nEstimated selling price $204,000\nEstimated cost of disposal 10,000\nNormal profit margin 30,000\nCurrent replacement cost 180,000\nThe company records losses that result from applying the\nlower-of-cost-or-market rule. At December 31, the loss that the\ncompany should recognize is","options":["$0","$6,000","$14,000","$20,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Inventory accounted for using LIFO or the retail inventory\nmethod is measured at the lower of cost or market (LCM). As\nindicated below, the $180,000 replacement cost falls between\nthe $194,000 ceiling and the $164,000 floor. Thus, it will be\nused as market in the LCM determination. Because the\n$180,000 market value is $20,000 lower than the $200,000\nhistorical cost, the inventory should be valued at $180,000 and\na $20,000 loss recognized.\nFact Pattern: Stockholm Co. accounts for its inventory using the first\nin, first out (FIFO) method. The data below concern items in\nStockholm Co.’s inventory.\nPer Unit Gear Stuff Wicket\ns\nHistorical cost $190.0\n0\n$106.0\n0\n$53.00\nSelling price 217.00 145.00 73.75\nCost to complete and\nsell\n19.00 8.00 2.50\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\nCurrent replacement\ncost\n203.00 105.00 51.00\nNormal profit margin 32.00 29.00 21.25","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q296","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":4,"question":"4) Under IFRS, what amount should Stockholm Co. compare with\nhistorical cost to measure the amount at which the wickets\nshould be measured?","options":["$51.00","$50.00","$71.25","$73.75\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Inventory is recorded at cost. However, inventory must be\nwritten down if its utility is no longer as great as its cost. To\nmake this determination under IFRS, historical cost is\ncompared with the inventory’s net realizable value (NRV).\nNRV is the estimated selling price in the ordinary course of\nbusiness minus estimated costs of completion and sale. NRV\nfor the wickets is $71.25 ($73.75 selling price – $2.50\nestimated costs of completion and sale).","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q297","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":5,"question":"5) An entity that prepares its financial statements using IFRS\nreported the following selected per-unit data relating to work-\nin-process:\nSelling price $100\nCompletion costs 10\nHistorical cost 91\nReplacement cost 108\nNormal gross profit 20\nSelling cost 5\nIn comparison with historical cost, what will be the per-unit\neffect on gross profit of measuring ending inventory?","options":["No effect.","Reduction of $6.","Reduction of $26.","Increase of $5."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Under IFRS, inventories are measured subsequent to\nacquisition at the lower of cost or net realizable value (NRV).\nNRV equals selling price minus estimated completion and\nselling costs. Given that historical cost is $91 and NRV is $85\n($100 selling price – $10 completion cost – $5 selling cost),\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\nthe effect on per-unit gross profit is a reduction of $6. This\namount is expensed.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q298","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":6,"question":"6) Based on a physical inventory taken on December 31, a\ncompany determined its chocolate inventory on a LIFO basis\nat $26,000 with a replacement cost of $20,000. The company\nestimated that, after further processing costs of $12,000, the\nchocolate could be sold as finished candy bars for $40,000.\nThe company’s normal profit margin is 10% of sales. Under\nthe lower-of-cost-or-market rule, what amount should the\ncompany report as chocolate inventory in its December 31\nbalance sheet?","options":["$28,000","$26,000","$24,000","$20,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Market equals current replacement cost subject to maximum\nand minimum values. The maximum is NRV, and the minimum\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\nis NRV minus normal profit. When replacement cost is within\nthis range, it is used as market. Cost is given as $26,000.\nNRV is $28,000 ($40,000 selling price – $12,000 additional\nprocessing costs), and NRV minus a normal profit equals\n$24,000 [$28,000 – ($40,000 × 10%)]. Because the lowest\namount in the range ($24,000) exceeds replacement cost\n($20,000), it is used as market. Because market value\n($24,000) is less than cost ($26,000), it is also the inventory\namount.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q299","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":7,"question":"7) Under the lower-of-cost-or-market method, the replacement\ncost of an inventory item would be used as the designated\nmarket value","options":["When it is below the net realizable value less the normal profit\nmargin.","When it is below the net realizable value and above the net\nrealizable value less the normal profit margin.","When it is above the net realizable value.","Regardless of net realizable value."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Market is current replacement cost subject to maximum and\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\nminimum values. The maximum is net realizable value, and\nthe minimum is net realizable value less normal profit. When\nreplacement cost is within this range, it is used as the market\namount.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q300","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":8,"question":"8) The replacement cost of an inventory item is below the net\nrealizable value and above the net realizable value less the\nnormal profit margin. The original cost of the inventory item is\nbelow the net realizable value less the normal profit margin.\nUnder the lower-of-cost-or-market (LCM) method, the\ninventory item should be valued at","options":["Net realizable value.","Net realizable value less the normal profit margin.","Original cost.","Replacement cost."],"answer":"C","explanation":"When replacement cost is below the NRV and above the NRV\nless the normal profit margin, market equals replacement cost.\nGiven that the original cost of the inventory item is below\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\nmarket, the original cost should be used to measure the\ninventory item under the LCM method.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q301","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":9,"question":"9) A firm has determined its fiscal year-end inventory on a LIFO\nbasis to be $400,000. Information pertaining to that inventory\nfollows:\nEstimated selling price $408,000\nEstimated cost of disposal 20,000\nNormal profit margin 60,000\nCurrent replacement cost 360,000\nThe firm records losses that result from applying the lower-of-\ncost-or-market (LCM) rule. At its year end, what should be the\nnet carrying value of the firm’s inventory?","options":["$400,000","$388,000","$360,000","$328,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Inventory accounted for using LIFO or the retail inventory\nmethod is measured at the lower of cost or market (LCM).\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\nUnder the LCM method, market is current replacement cost\nsubject to a maximum (ceiling) equal to net realizable value\nand a minimum (floor) equal to net realizable value minus a\nnormal profit. NRV equals selling price minus costs of\ncompletion and disposal. Here, original cost is $400,000 and\nreplacement cost is $360,000. The LCM method uses the\nlower of the two, $360,000, to measure inventory. However,\nthe inventory measure cannot exceed the NRV of $388,000\n($408,000 selling price – $20,000 cost of disposal).\nFurthermore, the inventory carrying amount cannot be lower\nthan NRV minus normal profit, or $328,000 ($388,000 NRV –\n$60,000 normal profit). Because the lower of cost or market\n($360,000) is between $388,000 (ceiling) and $328,000 (floor),\nthe net carrying amount is $360,000.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q302","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":10,"question":"10) Which of the following is true regarding inventory adjustments\nunder IFRS?","options":["IFRS do not require inventory adjustments.","Reversals of adjustments are allowed in a subsequent period.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition","A reversal of a previous write-down may be higher than the\nprevious write-down.","Adjustments may not be reversed in a subsequent period."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Both IFRS and U.S. GAAP require the cost of inventory to be\nwritten down if the utility of the goods is impaired. Under IFRS,\ninventories are measured subsequent to initial recognition at\nthe lower of cost and net realizable value (NRV), with NRV\nassessed each period. Moreover, unlike U.S. GAAP, IFRS\npermit inventory to be written up to the lower of cost and NRV\nif previously written down. The reversal is permissible only to\nthe extent of the prior write-down.","sectionId":"2.4"},{"id":"v1-su2-q303","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":11,"question":"11) A multinational company maintains its financial records under\nboth IFRS and U.S. GAAP. Last year, the company\ndetermined its inventory was impaired because demand for its\nproduct collapsed when a competitor launched a new product\nwith innovative features. As a result, the company wrote down\nits inventory to $0 with a carrying amount of $500,000. This\nyear, however, government authorities unexpectedly\nannounced that the competitor’s product was defective and the\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\nproduct was removed from the market. As a result, the\ncompany’s products were again in demand and the company\nestimated their net realizable value to be $750,000 at the end\nof the current quarter. How should the company record this\nnew development in the current quarter?","options":["Under IFRS, $0 write-up of the inventory; under U.S. GAAP,\n$0 write-up of the inventory.","Under IFRS, $500,000 write-up of the inventory; under U.S.\nGAAP, $0 write-up of the inventory.","Under IFRS, $750,000 write-up of the inventory; under U.S.\nGAAP, $0 write-up of the inventory.","Under IFRS, $750,000 write-up of the inventory; under U.S.\nGAAP, $750,000 write-up of the inventory.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Under U.S. GAAP, write-downs taken to reduce inventories\ncan not be reversed for subsequent increases in value. Under\nIFRS, write-downs taken to reduce inventories can be\nreversed for subsequent increases in value, but the reversal is\nlimited to the amount of the original write-down.","sectionId":"2.4"},{"id":"v1-su2-q304","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":12,"question":"12) A company uses IFRS to value its inventory of frozen foods.\nThe company applies IFRS on a total inventory basis, not\ndirectly to each item of frozen food. Information on the frozen\nfood inventory at December 31 of the year just ended is\nprovided below.\nReplacement cost $ 80,000\nNet realizable value less profit margin 85,000\nWeighted-average cost 90,000\nNet realizable value 100,000\nThe company should value its inventory at","options":["$80,000","$85,000","$90,000","$100,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Under IFRS, inventories are measured at the lower of cost or\nnet realizable value (NRV). Thus, the company should value\nits inventory at $90,000.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q305","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":13,"question":"13) A fundamental difference between U.S. GAAP and IFRS is\nthat","options":["Reversal of inventory write-downs is permitted under IFRS;\nhowever, reversal of inventory write-downs is prohibited under\nU.S. GAAP.","Distribution costs are included in cost of sales under U.S.\nGAAP; however, distribution costs are excluded from cost of\nsales under IFRS.","Inventory is generally valued at the lower of cost or market\nunder IFRS; however, inventory is generally valued at the\nlower of cost or net realizable value under U.S. GAAP.","Marketing costs are included in cost of sales under U.S.\nGAAP; however, marketing costs are excluded from cost of\nsales under IFRS."],"answer":"A","explanation":"A reversal of inventory write-downs is permitted under IFRS\nbut is prohibited under U.S. GAAP.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q306","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":14,"question":"14) The replacement cost of a LIFO basis inventory item is below\nthe net realizable value and above the net realizable value\nminus the normal profit margin. The original cost of the\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\ninventory item is below the net realizable value minus the\nnormal profit margin. Under the lower-of-cost-or-market (LCM)\nmethod, the inventory item should be measured at","options":["Net realizable value.","Net realizable value minus the normal profit margin.","Original cost.","Replacement cost."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Inventory accounted for using LIFO or the retail inventory\nmethod is measured at the lower of cost or market. When\nreplacement cost is below the NRV and above the NRV minus\nthe normal profit margin, market equals replacement cost.\nGiven that the original cost of the inventory item is below\nmarket, the original cost should be used to measure the\ninventory item under the LCM method.","sectionId":"2.4"},{"id":"v1-su2-q307","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":15,"question":"15) The original cost of an inventory item is both below\nreplacement cost and net realizable value. The net realizable\nvalue minus normal profit margin is below the original cost.\nUnder the lower-of-cost-or-market (LCM) method, the\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\ninventory item that is accounted for using the LIFO method\nshould be measured at","options":["Replacement cost.","Net realizable value.","Net realizable value minus normal profit margin.","Original cost."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Inventory accounted for using LIFO or the retail inventory\nmethod is measured at the lower of cost or market. Market is\ncurrent replacement cost subject to a ceiling and a floor. The\nmaximum is net realizable value, and the minimum is net\nrealizable value minus normal profit. When replacement cost\nis within this range, it is used as market. The original cost is\nabove the NRV minus normal profit margin but below the NRV\nand the replacement cost. Thus, market must be the NRV (if it\nis less than replacement cost) or the replacement cost (which\nis greater than NRV minus normal profit), and LCM is equal to\noriginal cost.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\nFact Pattern: Stockholm Co. accounts for its inventory using the last-\nin, first-out (LIFO) method. The data below concern items in\nStockholm Co.’s inventory.\nPer Unit Gear Stuff Wickets\nHistorical cost $190.00 $106.00 $53.00\nSelling price 217.00 145.00 73.75\nCost to\ncomplete and\nsell\n19.00 8.00 2.50\nCurrent\nreplacement\ncost\n203.00 105.00 51.00\nNormal profit\nmargin\n32.00 29.00 21.25","sectionId":"2.4"},{"id":"v1-su2-q308","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":16,"question":"16) The limits to the market measurement (i.e., the ceiling and the\nfloor) that Stockholm Co. should use in the lower-of-cost-or-\nmarket (LCM) comparison of gear are","options":["$217 and $198.","$217 and $185.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition","$198 and $166.","$185 and $166."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Inventory accounted for using LIFO or the retail inventory\nmethod is measured at the lower of cost or market (LCM).\nMarket is current replacement cost subject to a ceiling equal to\nnet realizable value and a floor equal to net realizable value\nminus a normal profit. Net realizable value is the estimated\nselling price minus costs of completion and disposal. For gear,\nthe net realizable value is $198 ($217 selling price –\n$19 completion and disposal costs). Net realizable value\nminus normal profit is $166 ($198 net realizable value –\n$32 normal profit).","sectionId":"2.4"},{"id":"v1-su2-q309","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":17,"question":"17) The cost amount that Stockholm Co. should use in the lower-\nof-cost-or-market (LCM) comparison of stuff is","options":["$105","$106","$108","$137"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Inventory accounted for using LIFO or the retail inventory\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\nmethod is measured at the LCM. Market is the current cost to\nreplace the inventory, subject to certain limitations. Market\nshould not exceed a ceiling equal to net realizable value\n(NRV) or be less than a floor equal to NRV minus normal profit\nmargin. NRV for stuff is $137 ($145 – $8). NRV minus normal\nprofit margin is $108 ($137 – $29). The current replacement\ncost is below the floor of $108; therefore, the market is $108.\nBecause the historical cost of $106 is lower than the market of\n$108, historical cost should be the basis of valuation for stuff.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q310","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":18,"question":"18) The market amount that Stockholm Co. should use to measure\nthe wickets on the basis of the lower-of-cost-or-market (LCM)\nrule is","options":["$51.00","$53.00","$50.00","$71.25"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Inventory accounted for using LIFO or retail inventory method\nis measured at the lower of cost or market (LCM). Market is\nthe current cost to replace the inventory, subject to certain\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\nlimitations. Market should not exceed a ceiling equal to net\nrealizable value (NRV) or be less than a floor equal to NRV\nminus normal profit margin. Net realizable value for wickets is\n$71.25 ($73.75 selling price – $2.50 completion and disposal\ncosts). The net realizable value minus normal profit is $50\n($71.25 net realizable value – $21.25 normal profit margin).\nThe $51 replacement cost falls between the $71.25 ceiling\nand the $50 floor and is the appropriate market value.\nBecause the $51 market value is lower than the $53 historical\ncost, it should be the basis of valuation for the wickets.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q311","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":19,"question":"19) The original cost of an inventory item is above the replacement\ncost. The inventory item’s replacement cost is above the net\nrealizable value. Under the lower-of-cost-or-market method,\nthe inventory item accounted for using the retail inventory\nmethod should be valued at","options":["Original cost.","Replacement cost.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition","Net realizable value.","Net realizable value less normal profit margin."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Inventory accounted for using LIFO or the retail inventory\nmethod is measured at the lower of cost or market. Market is\nthe current cost to replace inventory, subject to certain\nlimitations. Market should not exceed a ceiling equal to net\nrealizable value (NRV) or be less than a floor equal to NRV\nminus a normal profit margin. Because replacement cost\nexceeds NRV, the ceiling is NRV.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q312","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":20,"question":"20) Inventory accounted for under which of the following cost flow\nmethods is not measured at the lower of cost or net realizable\nvalue?","options":["First-in, first-out (FIFO).","Moving average.","Last-in, first-out (LIFO).","Specific identification."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Inventory accounted for using LIFO or the retail inventory\nmethod is measured at the lower of cost or market (LCM).\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\nInventory accounted for using any other cost method (e.g.,\nFIFO or average cost) is measured at the lower of cost or net\nrealizable value (NRV).","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q313","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":21,"question":"21) A company accounts for its inventory using the first-in, first-out\n(FIFO) method. The following information pertains to the\ninventory at the end of the fiscal year:\nHistorical cost $150,000\nCurrent replacement cost 120,000\nNet realizable value (NRV) 125,000\nNormal profit margin 15,000\nFair value 140,000\nWhat amount should the company report as inventory on its\nyear-end balance sheet?","options":["$150,000","$125,000","$120,000","$140,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Inventory accounted for using the FIFO method (or any cost\nmethod other than LIFO or retail) is measured at the lower of\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\ncost or net realizable value (NRV). Because the NRV\n($125,000) is lower than the historical cost ($150,000), the\ninventory is reported on the year-end balance sheet at its NRV\nof $125,000.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q314","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":22,"question":"22) Lialia Co. has determined the cost of its fiscal year-end\nunfinished FIFO inventory to be $300,000. Information\npertaining to that inventory at year-end is as follows:\nEstimated selling price $330,000\nEstimated cost of disposal 20,000\nNormal profit margin 15%\nCurrent replacement cost 280,000\nEstimated completion costs 15,000\nWhat amount should Lialia report as inventory on its year-end\nbalance sheet?","options":["$295,000","$280,000","$300,000","$330,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Inventory accounted for using the FIFO method (or any cost\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\nmethod other than LIFO or retail) is measured at the lower of\ncost or net realizable value (NRV). NRV is the estimated\nselling price in the ordinary course of business, minus\nreasonably predictable costs of completion, disposal, and\ntransportation. At year-end, the NRV of the inventory of\n$295,000 ($330,000 estimated selling price – $15,000\nestimated completion costs – $20,000 estimated costs of\ndisposal) is lower than its cost of $300,000. Thus, the\ninventory is reported at its NRV of $295,000.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q315","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":23,"question":"23) Gail Co. has determined the cost of its 12/31/Year 1 inventory\non a moving-average basis to be $200,000. Information\npertaining to that inventory at year-end is as follows:\nEstimated selling price $215,000\nEstimated cost of disposal 10,000\nNormal profit margin 20,000\nCurrent replacement cost 190,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\nWhat loss on inventory write-down, if any, should be\nrecognized in Gail’s Year 1 income statement?","options":["$10,000","$15,000","$20,000","$0"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Inventory accounted for using the average method (or any\ncost method other than LIFO or retail) is measured at the\nlower of cost or net realizable value (NRV). NRV is the\nestimated selling price in the ordinary course of business,\nminus reasonably predictable costs of completion, disposal,\nand transportation. The cost of the inventory of $200,000 is\nlower than its NRV of $205,000 ($215,000 selling price –\n$10,000 estimated cost of disposal). Thus, the inventory is\nreported at its cost and no loss on write-down of inventory is\nrecognized.","sectionId":"2.4"},{"id":"v1-su2-q316","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":24,"question":"24) How should inventory that is accounted for under the average-\ncost method be measured in the financial statements?","options":["Lower of cost or market (LCM).\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition","Lower of cost or fair value minus cost to sell.","Lower of cost or net realizable value (NRV).","Fair value."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Inventory accounted for using the average-cost method (or\nany cost method other than LIFO or retail) is measured at the\nlower of cost or NRV. NRV is the estimated selling price in the\nordinary course of business, minus reasonably predictable\ncosts of completion, disposal, and transportation.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q317","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":25,"question":"25) A company determined the following values for its inventory as\nof the end of the fiscal year:\nHistorical cost $300,000\nCurrent replacement cost 280,000\nSelling price 308,000\nNormal profit margin 13,000\nCost to sell 10,000\nWhat amount should the company report as inventory on its\nyear-end balance sheet under the following cost methods?\nLast-in, first-out (LIFO) First-in, first-out (FIFO)\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition","options":["$280,000 $298,000","$285,000 $298,000","$298,000 $300,000","$285,000 $285,000"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Inventory accounted for using LIFO or the retail inventory\nmethod is measured at the lower of cost or market (LCM).\nMarket is the current cost to replace inventory, subject to\ncertain limitations. Market cannot be greater than a ceiling\nequal to net realizable value (NRV) of $298,000\n($308,000 estimated selling price – $10,000 cost of disposal).\nIt cannot be less than a floor equal to NRV reduced by a\nnormal profit margin of $285,000 ($298,000 NRV – $13,000\nnormal profit margin). Because the current replacement cost\n($280,000) is lower than the floor ($285,000), market is\n$285,000. Thus, under the LIFO method, the inventory is\nreported at $285,000, which is lower than the $300,000 cost.\nInventory accounted for using the FIFO method (or any cost\nmethod other than LIFO or retail) is measured at the lower of\ncost or net realizable value (NRV). Thus, under FIFO method,\nthe inventory is reported at its NRV of $298,000, which is\nlower than the $300,000 cost.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition","sectionId":"2.4"},{"id":"v1-su2-q318","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":26,"question":"26) In accounting for inventories, GAAP require departure from the\nhistorical cost principle when the utility of inventory has fallen\nbelow cost. Inventory accounted for under certain cost flow\nmethods can be measured at the lower of cost or net\nrealizable value (NRV). The term “net realizable value (NRV)”\nas defined here means","options":["Fair value minus cost to sell.","Original cost minus normal profit margin.","Estimated selling price minus estimated costs of completion\nand disposal.","Fair value minus normal profit margin.\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Inventory measured using any cost method other than LIFO or\nretail (e.g., FIFO or average cost) must be measured at the\nlower of cost or NRV. NRV is the estimated selling price in the\nordinary course of business, minus reasonably predictable\ncosts of completion, disposal, and transportation.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q319","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":27,"question":"27) Inventory accounted for under which cost flow methods is\nmeasured subsequent to acquisition at lower of cost or market\n(LCM)?\nLIFO FIFO Retail Weighted-Average","options":["Yes No Yes No","Yes Yes No No","No Yes No Yes","No No Yes Yes"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Inventory accounted for using LIFO or the retail inventory\nmethod is measured at the lower of cost or market (LCM).\nInventory accounted for using any other cost method (e.g.,\nFIFO, average cost, or specific identification) is measured at\nthe lower of cost or net realizable value (NRV).","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q320","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":28,"question":"28) Kusta Company measures its year-end inventory using the\nweighted-average method. The following is relevant\ninformation:\nCosts of completion, disposal, and transportation $ 25,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\nEstimated selling price 450,000\nCurrent replacement cost 420,000\nNormal profit margin 40,000\nCost 400,000\nThe amount of ending inventory reported in the statement of\nfinancial position is","options":["$400,000","$420,000","$425,000\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition","$450,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Inventory accounted for using a method other than LIFO or\nretail (e.g., FIFO, average cost, or specific identification) is\nmeasured subsequent to acquisition at lower of cost or net\nrealizable value (NRV). NRV equals (1) the estimated selling\nprice in the ordinary course of business minus (2) reasonably\npredictable costs of completion, disposal, and transportation.\nAccordingly, the year-end weighted-average inventory is\nmeasured at the lower of cost ($400,000) or NRV [($450,000\nsales price – $25,000 costs to complete, etc.) = $425,000],\nthat is, the $400,000 historical cost.","sectionId":"2.4"},{"id":"v1-su2-q321","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":29,"question":"29) A company determined the following values for its inventory as\nof the end of the fiscal year:\nHistorical cost $100,000\nCurrent replacement cost 70,000\nNet realizable value 90,000\nNet realizable value minus a normal profit margin 85,000\nFair value 95,000\nIf the company accounts for its inventories using the first-in\nfirst-out (FIFO) cost method, what amount should the company\nreport as inventory on its balance sheet?","options":["$70,000","$85,000","$90,000","$95,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Under FIFO, inventory is measured at the lower of cost or\nNRV (Estimated selling price in the ordinary course of\nbusiness – Estimated costs of completion and sale). Given\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\ncost of $100,000 and NRV of $90,000, inventory should be\nreported at $90,000.","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q322","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":30,"question":"30) Garcel, Inc., held unfinished LIFO inventory at a cost of\n$85,000 with a sales value of $125,000. The inventory will cost\n$10,500 to complete. The normal profit margin is 30% of sales.\nThe replacement cost of the inventory was $75,000. What\namount should Garcel report as inventory on the balance\nsheet?","options":["$114,500","$85,000","$77,000","$75,000"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Inventory accounted for using LIFO or the retail inventory\nmethod is measured at the lower of cost or market (LCM).\nMarket is the current cost to replace inventory, subject to\ncertain limitations. Market should not (1) exceed a ceiling\nequal to net realizable value (NRV) or (2) be less than a floor\nequal to NRV reduced by an allowance for an approximately\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\nnormal profit margin. NRV is the estimated selling price in the\nordinary course of business minus reasonably predictable\ncosts of completion and disposal. Thus, NRV (ceiling) equals\n$114,500 ($125,000 selling price – $10,500 cost of\ncompletion). If the normal profit margin equals $37,500\n($125,000 selling price × 30% normal profit margin), the floor\nequals $77,000 ($114,500 NRV – $37,500 normal profit\nmargin). Because the replacement cost of inventory ($75,000)\nis lower than the floor ($77,000), market is $77,000.\nAccordingly, the LIFO inventory must be written down to\nmarket and reported at $77,000 because market is lower than\ncost ($85,000).","sectionId":"2.3"},{"id":"v1-su2-q323","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":31,"question":"31) The following information relates to a company’s year-end\ninventory:\nInventory cost $910\nSelling price of inventory $1,000\nNormal profit margin 10% of selling price\nCurrent replacement cost $740\nCost of completion and disposal $100\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\nUnder the first-in first-out (FIFO) cost method, what is the\ncompany’s year-end inventory balance?","options":["$740","$800","$900","$910\n3"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Under FIFO, inventories are measured at the lower of cost or\nnet realizable value (NRV). NRV is the estimated selling price\nless the estimated costs of completion and disposal. The\ninventory cost is $910. The NRV is $900 ($1,000 – $100). The\nlower of the two is $900. Therefore, the company’s year-end\ninventory balance is $900.","sectionId":"2.4"},{"id":"v1-su2-q324","volume":1,"unit":2,"unitTitle":"Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure: Assets — Short-Term","subunit":null,"questionNumber":2,"question":"2)\nA company manufactures and distributes replacement parts for\nvarious industries. As of December 31, Year 1, the following\namounts pertain to the company’s inventory:\nIte\nm Cost\nNet\nReplace\nment\nCost\nSale\nPrice\nCost to\nSell\nor\nNormal\nProfit Ma\nrgin\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\nDispos\ne\nBlades $41,000 $ 38,000 $ 50,000 $ 2,000 $15,000\nTowers 52,000 40,000 54,000 4,000 14,000\nGener\nators\n20,000 24,000 30,000 2,000 6,000\nGearb\noxes\n80,000 105,000 120,000 12,000 8,000\nWhat is the total carrying value of the company’s inventory as of\nDecember 31, Year 1, under the weighted-average cost\nmethod?","options":["$178,000","$191,000","$193,000","$207,000\n\nLong-Term Items | Subunit 1: Investments in Equity Securities\n3: (125) Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure:\nAssets -- Long-Term Items\n1: (7) Investments in Equity Securities\n2: (19) Equity Method\n3: (27) Investments in Debt Securities\n4: (50) Property, Plant, and Equipment\n5: (22) Intangible Assets\n3: (125) Measurement, Valuation, and Disclosure:\nAssets -- Long-Term Items\n1: (7) Investments in Equity Securities\nLong-Term Items | Subunit 1: Investments in Equity Securities"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Under the weighted-average cost method, inventories are\nmeasured at the lower of cost or net realizable value (NRV).\nNRV is the estimated selling price minus reasonably predictable\ncosts of completion and disposal. Blades, generators, and\ngearboxes are measured at their costs of $41,000, $20,000, and\n$80,000, respectively, because these amounts are lower than\n\nShort-Term Items | Subunit 4: Measurement of Inventory\nSubsequent to Initial Recognition\ntheir NRVs of $48,000 ($50,000 – $2,000), $28,000 ($30,000 –\n$2,000), and $108,000 ($120,000 – $12,000). Towers are\nmeasured at their NRV of $50,000 ($54,000 – $4,000) because\nthis amount is lower than cost ($52,000). Thus, the total carrying\nvalue of the company’s inventory is equal to $191,000\n($41,000 + $20,000 + $80,000 + $50,000).","sectionId":"2.4"},{"id":"tb.recv.006_1805","question":"Unlike the allowance method, the direct write-off method:","options":["Is forbidden by GAAP because it incorporates the matching principle.","Is forbidden by GAAP because it does not incorporate the matching principle.","Is required by GAAP because it does not incorporate the matching principle.","Is required by GAAP because it incorporates the matching principle."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Under the direct write-off method bad debt expense is recorded and accounts receivable written off when a company learns that a specific receivable will not be paid. This method violates the matching principle because bad debt is not recorded at the same time the associated revenue is recorded. This makes it unacceptable for financial reporting. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.rec.003_1802","question":"A method of estimating bad debts that focuses on the income statement rather than the balance sheet is the allowance method based on:","options":["Direct write-off.","Aging the trade receivable accounts.","Credit sales.","The balance in the trade receivable accounts."],"answer":"C","explanation":"This answer is correct. Estimating bad debts based on credit sales of the period is the income statement approach in that bad debts are treated as a function of sales.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.recv.011_1805","question":"Which of the following statements is true about accounting for uncollectible accounts under the allowance method of accounting?","options":["The cash realizable value of accounts receivable is greater before an account is written off than after it is written off.","Bad Debt Expense is debited when a specific account is written off as doubtful.","The cash realizable value of accounts receivable in the balance sheet is the same before and after an account is written off.","Allowance for Doubtful Accounts is closed each year to Income Summary."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Under the allowance method an estimate is \\made of the dollar value of accounts receivable that will not be collected. This amount is the basis of bad debt expense. When an account is written off, the allowance for doubtful accounts is reduced (debited) and gross accounts receivable is reduced (credited) by the same amount. This means the cash realizable value is the same after the account is written off as it is before it is written off. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.rec.004_1802","question":"When a specific customer's account receivable is written off as uncollectible, what will be the effect on net income under the allowance method and the direct write-off method of recognizing bad debt expense?","options":["None, Decreased","Decreased, None","Decreased, Decreased","None, None"],"answer":"A","explanation":"This answer is correct because under the allowance method a bad debt is written off by making the following entry: Since neither of the accounts involved in this entry are income statement accounts, there can be no income statement effect. Under the direct write-off method, the entry to write off a bad debt would be Since the bad debt expense account is an income statement account, the write-off will cause net income to decrease.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.recv.015_1805","question":"Both Fowler Landscaping and Stanley Cleaning Services have estimated their uncollectible accounts for 20x8 to be $3,500. In addition, both companies use the allowance method for uncollectible accounts. If Stanley Cleaning Services has to record a larger adjusting entry for their Allowance for Doubtful Accounts account at the end of 20x7, what can you assume about the balance of the Allowance for Doubtful Accounts account for each company?","options":["Stanley Cleaning Services has a credit balance in Allowance for Doubtful Accounts at the end of 20x7, whereas Fowler Landscaping has a debit balance.","Stanley Cleaning Services has a zero balance in Allowance for Doubtful Accounts at the end of 20x7, whereas Fowler Landscaping has a debit balance.","Stanley Cleaning Services has a debit balance in Allowance for Doubtful Accounts at the end of 20x7, whereas Fowler Landscaping has a credit balance.","Stanley Cleaning Services has a credit balance in Allowance for Doubtful Accounts at the end of 20x7, whereas Fowler Landscaping has a zero balance."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Under the allowance method an estimate is made of the dollar value of accounts receivable that will not be collected. Bad debt expense is the amount needed to make the balance in the allowance for doubtful accounts equal to the estimate of the dollar value of accounts receivable that will not be collected. The “normal” balance in the allowance for doubtful accounts is a credit balance. A larger entry for Stanley to reach an ending balance of $3,500 means its allowance balance prior to adjustment is a smaller credit balance than Fowler's, a debit balance, while Fowler's is a credit balance, or a larger debit balance than Fowler's debit balance. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.recv.016_1805","question":"When using the allowance method, what should a firm do?","options":["Debit estimated uncollectibles to both Bad Debt Expense and Allowance for Doubtful accounts through an adjusting entry at the end of each period.","Debit estimated uncollectibles to Bad Debt Expense and credit them to Allowance for Doubtful accounts through an adjusting entry at the end of each accounting period.","Credit estimated uncollectibles to Bad Debt Expense and debit them to Allowance for Doubtful accounts through an adjusting entry at the end of each period.","Credit estimated uncollectibles to both Bad Debt Expense and Allowance for Doubtful accounts through an adjusting entry at the end of each period."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Under the allowance method an estimate is made of the dollar value of accounts receivable that will not be collected. This amount is used to calculate bad debt expense for a period. The journal entry is a debit to bad debt expense and a credit to allowance for doubtful accounts. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.recv.010_1805","question":"What will happen if the amount of bad debt expense is understated at year-end?","options":["Net income will be understated.","Net accounts receivable will be overstated.","Stockholders’ equity will be understated.","Allowance for Doubtful Accounts will be overstated."],"answer":"B","explanation":"When bad debt expense is recorded the off-setting credit is to allowance for doubtful accounts. The allowance is needed to reduce the net realizable value of accounts receivable to the amount expected to be collected. If bad debt expense is understated, the allowance for doubtful is understated. This results in the net accounts receivable being overstated. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.recv.002_1805","question":"Which of the following is an application of the allowance for doubtful accounts—which appears as a deduction from accounts receivable on a balance sheet and which is based on an estimate of bad debts?","options":["Consistency characteristic","Materiality quality","Revenue recognition principle","Expense recognition principle"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Allowance for doubtful accounts is the off-setting credit when bad debt expense is debited. This journal entry is based on an estimate of the amount of uncollectible accounts, not actual uncollectible accounts. An estimate is used in order to be consistent with the expense recognition principle, which states that expenses should be recognized in the period incurred or when the associated revenue is recorded. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.rec.007_1802","question":"Rue Co.’s allowance for uncollectible accounts had a credit balance of $12,000 at December 31, Year 1. During Year 2, Rue wrote off uncollectible accounts of $48,000. Using the balance sheet approach, the aging of accounts receivable indicated that a $50,000 allowance for uncollectible accounts was required at December 31, Year 2. What amount of uncollectible accounts expense should Rue report for Year 2?","options":["$48,000","$50,000","$60,000","$86,000"],"answer":"D","explanation":"This answer is correct. An examination of the T-account indicates the beginning balance in allowance for uncollectible accounts has a credit balance of $12,000. When an account is written off, a debit entry is made to the allowance for uncollectible accounts. If the aging schedule indicates that a $50,000 allowance is required, then the ending balance in the Allowance for uncollectible accounts must be $50,000. Therefore, this answer is correct because $86,000 ($12,000 − $48,000 − $50,000) must be debited to bad debt expense and credited to the allowance for uncollectible accounts.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.recv.001_1805","question":"When a company collects on an account after writing it off as uncollectible, it makes one journal entry:","options":["Reversing the entry made when writing off the account","Reversing the entry made when writing off the account, and another entry recording the collection in the usual manner","Recording the collection in the usual manner","Charging the amount to Bad Debt Expense, and other entry recording the collection in the usual manner"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Writing off an accounts receivable requires a journal entry to remove the receivable (credit) and decrease the allowance for doubtful accounts (debit). When an account that was previously written off is collected, a journal entry reinstating the account must be made. This is a reverse of the entry to write off the account. A second entry recording the collection in the usual manner is also made. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.recv.012_1805","question":"Under the allowance method, what happens when a specific account is written off?","options":["Total assets will be unchanged.","Net income will decrease.","Total assets will decrease.","Total assets will increase."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Under the allowance method an estimate is made of the dollar value of accounts receivable that will not be collected. This amount is the basis of bad debt expense. When an account is written off, the allowance for doubtful accounts is reduced (debited) and gross accounts receivable is reduced (credited) by the same amount. This means the cash realizable value is the same after the account is written off as it is before it is written off. This means total assets will be unchanged when a specific account is written off. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.recv.024_1805","question":"If the allowance method is used to account for uncollectible accounts, when is bad debt expense debited?","options":["When a sale is made","When management estimates the amount of uncollectibles","When an account becomes bad and is written off","When a customer's account becomes past due"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Under the allowance method an estimate is made of the dollar value of accounts receivable that will not be collected. This amount is used to calculate bad debt expense for a period. The journal entry is a debit to bad debt expense and a credit to allowance for doubtful accounts. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.recv.014_1805","question":"Newman Shoes and Bowman Footwear both decided to write off a specific customer's uncollectible account as a bad debt expense. If Newman Shoes uses the direct write-off method and Bowman Footwear uses the allowance method for uncollectible accounts, what will be the difference in the journal entries for these two companies?","options":["Newman Shoes will record a credit to Bad Debt Expense, whereas Bowman Footwear will record a credit to Allowance for Doubtful Accounts.","Newman Shoes will record a debit to Allowance for Doubtful Accounts, whereas Bowman Footwear will record a debit to Bad Debt Expense.","Newman Shoes will record a debit to Bad Debt Expense, whereas Bowman Footwear will record a debit to Allowance for Doubtful Accounts.","Newman Shoes will record a credit to Allowance for Doubtful Accounts, whereas Bowman Footwear will record a credit to Bad Debt Expense."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Under the allowance method an estimate is made of the dollar value of accounts receivable that will not be collected. This allowance account reduces the net realizable value of accounting receivable. When an account is written off, the allowance for doubtful accounts is reduced (debited) and gross accounts receivable is reduced (credited) by the same amount. Under the direct write-off method, bad debt expense is only recorded when a specific account is written off. The journal entry consists of a debit to bad debt expense and a credit to accounts receivable. If Newman is using the direct write-off method it would debit bad debt expense and Bowman would debit allowance for doubtful accounts when a specific account is written off. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.recv.005_1805","question":"Because the ________ method violates the ________, it is unacceptable for financial reporting.","options":["direct write-off; matching principle","direct write-off; going-concern principle","allowance; matching principle","allowance; relevance criteria"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Under the direct write-off method bad debt expense is recorded and accounts receivable written off when a company learns that a specific receivable will not be paid. This method violates the matching principle because bad debt is not recorded at the same time the associated revenue is recorded. This makes it unacceptable for financial reporting. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.recv.009_1805","question":"What happens if a company fails to record estimated bad debt expense?","options":["Cash realizable value is understated.","Revenues are understated.","Receivables are understated.","Expenses are understated."],"answer":"D","explanation":"If bad debt expense is not recorded, total expenses are understated. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.recv.020_1805","question":"Using the percentage-of-receivables basis, First Manufacturing estimates it will have total bad debts of $8,235 in the coming year. If First's trial balance shows an Allowance for Doubtful Accounts with a credit balance of $750, which of the following adjusting entries should the firm include on its balance sheet?","options":["Bad Debt Expense =Debit of $8,985; Allowance for Doubtful Accounts = Credit of $8,985","Bad Debt Expense = Debit of $8,235; Allowance for Doubtful Accounts = Credit of $8,235","Bad Debt Expense = Debit of $7,485; Allowance for Doubtful Accounts = Credit of $7,485","Allowance for Doubtful Accounts = Debit of $8,985; Bad Debt Expense = Credit of $8,985"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Under the allowance method an estimate is made of the dollar value of accounts receivable that will not be collected. An aging of receivables analysis is one way to determine this amount. The result of the analysis is the credit balance needed in allowance for doubtful accounts after bad debt is recorded. Since there is a credit balance of $750 in allowance for doubtful accounts prior to recording bad debt expense, $7,485 must be added to allowance for doubtful accounts to make the balance a credit of $8,235. The journal entry is a debit to bad debt expense and a credit to allowance for doubtful accounts. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.rec.009_1802","question":"Steve's Igneous Rock store has generous credit terms, so it has significant amounts of accounts receivable (AR) outstanding. It has created the following aging analysis of its accounts receivable at year-end:","options":["$4,000","$3,500","$1,500","$12,500"],"answer":"B","explanation":"This answer is correct. This answer calculates the bad debt expense under the sales method as $12,500 = $250,000 × 5%. The bad debt expense under the aging method is computed properly. The ending balance under the aging method is $10,000 = $120,000 × 2% + $60,000 × 5% + $20,000 × 10% + $10,000 × 26%. Given the beginning $1,000 credit balance, an expense of $9,000 ($10,000 − $1,000) is needed to have the ending balance equal $10,000. Thus, the difference between the two is calculated as $3,500 = $12,500 − $9,000.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.recv.013_1805","question":"O'Brien Brewery and Delgado Vintage Wines both decided to write off a specific customer's uncollectible account as a bad debt expense. To record this transaction, O'Brien Brewery recorded a debit to Bad Debt Expense, whereas Delgado Vintage Wines recorded a debit to Allowance for Doubtful Accounts. What is the difference between these two companies?","options":["O'Brien Brewery uses the direct write-off method for uncollectible accounts, whereas Delgado Vintage Wines uses the allowance method for uncollectible accounts.","O'Brien Brewery uses the allowance method for uncollectible accounts, whereas Delgado Vintage Wines uses the direct write-off method for uncollectible accounts.","O'Brien Brewery uses the factor write-off method for uncollectible accounts, whereas Delgado Vintage Wines uses the direct write-off method for uncollectible accounts.","O'Brien Brewery uses the allowance method for uncollectible accounts, whereas Delgado Vintage Wines uses the factor write-off method for uncollectible accounts."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Under the allowance method an estimate is made of the dollar value of accounts receivable that will not be collected. This allowance account reduces the net realizable value of accounting receivable. When an account is written off, the allowance for doubtful accounts is reduced (debited) and gross accounts receivable is reduced (credited) by the same amount. Under the direct write-off method, bad debt expense is only recorded when a specific account is written off. The journal entry consists of a debit to bad debt expense and a credit to accounts receivable. O'Brien is using the direct write-off method since it is debiting bad debt expense and Delgado is using the allowance method since it is debiting allowance for doubtful accounts when a specific account is written off. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.recv.018_1805","question":"Using the percentage-of-receivables basis, Continental Industries estimates it will have total bad debts of $25,150 in the coming year. If Continental's trial balance shows an Allowance for Doubtful Accounts with a debit balance of $8,750, which of the following adjusting entries should the firm include on its balance sheet?","options":["Bad Debt Expense = Debit of $16,400; Allowance for Doubtful Accounts = Credit of $16,400","Bad Debt Expense = Debit of $33,900; Allowance for Doubtful Accounts = Credit of $33,900","Bad Debt Expense = Debit of $25,150; Allowance for Doubtful Accounts = Credit of $25,150","Allowance for Doubtful Accounts = Debit of $16,400; Bad Debt Expense = Credit of $16,400"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Under the allowance method an estimate is made of the dollar value of accounts receivable that will not be collected. An aging of receivables analysis is one way to determine this amount. The result of the analysis is the credit balance needed in allowance for doubtful accounts after bad debt is recorded. Since there is a debit balance of $8,750 in allowance for doubtful accounts prior to recording bad debt expense, $33,900 must be added to allowance for doubtful accounts to make the balance a credit of $25,150. The journal entry is a debit to bad debt expense and a credit to allowance for doubtful accounts. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.recv.003_1805","question":"When should a merchandiser recognize an account receivable?","options":["At the point of sale","When cash is received","When payment is requested","When the goods are delivered"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Revenue is recognized when earned and realized (or realizable). Earned means that the seller has done substantially all it is required to do and realized or realizable means it has received cash or a claim to cash. For a merchandiser both of these typically happen at the point of sale as the seller provides the goods (earned) and receives cash or a claim to cash (selling on credit). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.recv.007_1805","question":"When recording bad debt expense under the allowance method, ________ is credited, and ________ is debited at the end of the accounting period.","options":["allowance for doubtful accounts; bad debt expense","bad debt expense; allowance for doubtful accounts","allowance for doubtful accounts; accounts receivable","accounts receivable; bad debt expense"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Under the allowance method an estimate is made of the dollar value of accounts receivable that will not be collected. This amount is used to calculate bad debt expense for a period. The journal entry is a debit to bad debt expense and a credit to allowance for doubtful accounts. This increases expenses and decreases assets. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.recv.008_1805","question":"How does the allowance method of accounting for bad debts provide better matching of expenses with revenues?","options":["By recording exactly the uncollectible accounts at the end of each period","By writing off uncollectible accounts at the end of each period","By estimating uncollectible accounts at the end of each period","By pursuing uncollectible accounts at the end of each period"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Under the allowance method an estimate is made of the dollar value of accounts receivable that will not be collected. This amount is the basis of bad debt expense. Since bad debt expense is recorded in the same period as the related revenue is recognized, this provides better matching of expenses with revenues than recording of bad debt expense when accounts become uncollectible (direct write-off method). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.rec.002_1802","question":"When the allowance method of recognizing bad debt expense is used, the allowance for doubtful accounts would decrease when a(n):","options":["Specific account receivable is collected.","Bad Debt Expense is recorded using the balance sheet approach.","Bad Debt Expense is recorded using the income statement approach.","Specific uncollectible account is written off."],"answer":"D","explanation":"This answer is correct. When the allowance method of recognizing bad debts is used, the entry to establish the allowance account is: The entry to write off a specific uncollectible account is Thus, the allowance is decreased when the account is written off.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.rec.001_1802","question":"A company is in its first year of operations and has never written off any accounts receivable as uncollectible. When the allowance method of recognizing bad debt expense is used, the entry to recognize that expense:","options":["Increases net income.","Decreases current assets.","Has no effect on current assets.","Has no effect on net income."],"answer":"B","explanation":"This answer is correct. When the allowance method of recognizing bad debt expense is used, the journal entry is: The allowance account is a contra account to accounts receivable. Since accounts receivable is a current asset, current assets will be decreased by the allowance for bad debts.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.rec.006_1802","question":"Elsa Fashions wants to eliminate its credit department. It also wants to get cash immediately and continue all operational activities directly with the customers. Which of the following approaches would be the best to fulfill the company's objectives?","options":["Factor without recourse","Securitization","Sale with recourse","Special investment vehicle"],"answer":"A","explanation":"This answer is correct. Companies that use factor without recourse get immediate cash and can eliminate their credit department, because the factor takes over these tasks. The company continues all operational activities directly with the customers, such as order placement and fulfillment.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.recv.004_1805","question":"When a sale is made on account, what impact does a sales discount have on the amount of cash eventually received?","options":["It decreases the amount of cash received.","It increases the amount of cash received.","It neither increases nor decreases the amount of cash received.","It either increases or decreases the amount of cash received in unpredictable ways."],"answer":"A","explanation":"A sales discount is an incentive offered to encourage early payment. It is typically stated as a percentage of the gross amount of the receivable. Since it is a discount on the gross amount owed, when debtors pay within the discount period, the creditor receives less cash. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.1","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.022_1805","question":"Karen and Ron are studying the financial information for two different corporations. In particular, they are looking at the difference in net income and gross profit between the two. One of the companies uses LIFO and the other uses FIFO. What changes between the two will Karen and Ron most likely notice?","options":["The company that uses LIFO will likely have a higher net income and gross profit than the company that uses FIFO.","The company that uses FIFO will likely have a higher net income and gross profit than the company that uses LIFO.","The company that uses FIFO will likely have a higher net income and a lower gross profit than the company that uses LIFO.","Both companies will likely have very similar net income and gross profit amounts, but their tax payments will vary widely."],"answer":"B","explanation":"FIFO uses the oldest prices when calculating cost of goods sold and the newest prices when calculating ending inventory. On the other hand, LIFO uses the newest prices when calculating cost of goods sold and the oldest prices when calculating ending inventory. Generally speaking, prices tend to increase over time. When prices are rising, cost of goods sold under FIFO is lower than under LIFO since it is calculated using older, lower prices. When cost of goods sold under FIFO is lower, net income and gross profit are higher since net sales and all other expenses (other than cost of goods sold) are the same under FIFO and LIFO. This means companies using FIFO tend to have higher net income and gross profit than do companies using LIFO. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.050_1805","question":"Kate's Company received merchandise on consignment. The company recorded the transaction as a purchase and included the goods in inventory, as of October 31. What would the effect of this be on the financial statements for October 31?","options":["There would be no effect.","The net income, current assets, and current liabilities would all be overstated.","The net income and current liabilities would be overstated.","The net income would be correct and current assets and current liabilities would be overstated."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Goods a company holds on consignment are not legally owned by the company. These items should be excluded from its inventory. If Kate records the receipt of the goods as a purchase, then it would debit “purchases” and credit “accounts payable.” This overstates current liabilities. If the goods are included in inventory on October 31, current assets are overstated since Kate does not legally own them. Counting the receipt of the goods as a purchase overstates cost of goods available for sale since purchases are added to beginning inventory to determine it. Since ending inventory is overstated by the same amount, cost of goods sold is correct. Since cost of goods sold is correct, net income is correct. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.036_1805","question":"If a company wishes to maximize net income, which inventory cost flow method would it use during inflationary periods?","options":["LIFO","Average-cost","FIFO","Specific identification"],"answer":"C","explanation":"FIFO uses the oldest prices when calculating cost of goods sold and the newest prices when calculating ending inventory. When prices are rising, cost of goods sold under FIFO is lower than under LIFO since it is calculated using older, lower prices. When cost of goods sold under FIFO is lower, net income is higher since net sales and all other expenses (other than cost of goods sold) are the same under FIFO and LIFO. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.013_1805","question":"Although the perpetual inventory system is used by most businesses, a year-end ________ is taken to reconcile actual inventory with inventory records.","options":["inventory count","gross profit","employee evaluation","closing entry for inventory"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Under a perpetual inventory system, the inventory account is updated whenever there is a transaction impacting inventory. Under this system, it is possible to know the inventory balance on any given day during the period. It is not necessary to conduct a physical count of inventory, although it can be used to reconcile actual inventory on hand with inventory records. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.021_1805","question":"Marci is looking over the profit and income information for two different companies. One company uses LIFO and the other uses FIFO. Prices are rising. What is Marci most likely to notice about these two companies?","options":["The company that uses LIFO will likely have a higher net income and gross profit than the company that uses FIFO.","The company that uses FIFO will likely have a higher net income and gross profit than the company that uses LIFO.","The company that uses FIFO will likely have a higher net income and a lower gross profit than the company that uses LIFO.","The company that uses FIFO will likely have a lower net income and a higher gross profit than the company that uses LIFO."],"answer":"B","explanation":"FIFO uses the oldest prices when calculating cost of goods sold and the newest prices when calculating ending inventory. On the other hand, LIFO uses the newest prices when calculating cost of goods sold and the oldest prices when calculating ending inventory. When prices are rising, cost of goods sold under FIFO is lower than under LIFO since it is calculated using older, lower prices. When cost of goods sold under FIFO is lower, net income and gross profit are higher since net sales and all other expenses (other than cost of goods sold) are the same under FIFO and LIFO. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.inv.005_1802","question":"Bowman Devices values its inventory using last in, first out (LIFO) method. For the current year, the inventory usage exceeded the purchases. Assuming inventory costs are falling, and all else is constant, how will this situation affect the income statement for the year?","options":["Taxes will be higher.","Net income will be lower.","Net income will be higher.","Cost of Goods Sold will be lower."],"answer":"B","explanation":"This answer is correct. If usage of goods exceeds purchases during a period, inventory levels are decreasing and older costs are passing through to COGS. If prices are falling, this results in higher COGS and lower income levels being reported and likely lower taxes as well.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.035_1805","question":"FIFO is used when prices are declining and companies wish to do which of the following?","options":["Minimize income taxes","Maximize income taxes","Adjust beginning inventory","Lower cost of goods"],"answer":"A","explanation":"FIFO uses the oldest prices when calculating cost of goods sold and the newest prices when calculating ending inventory. When prices are declining, cost of goods sold under FIFO is higher than under LIFO since it is calculated using older, higher prices. When cost of goods sold under FIFO is higher, income before taxes is lower since net sales and all other expenses (other than cost of goods sold) are the same under FIFO and LIFO. Lower income before taxes results in lower income tax expense. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.012_1805","question":"A physical count of inventory at the end of the accounting period is required under which inventory system(s)?","options":["Perpetual system","Periodic system","Periodic and perpetual systems","Sampling system"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Under a periodic inventory system, a company determines cost of goods sold only once at the end of the period. In addition, inventory purchases are debited to a “purchases” account. Since the inventory account is not updated during the period for purchases or sales, ending inventory needs to be determined by a physical inventory count. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.inv.011_1809","question":"Which of the following inventory cost flow assumptions would require the application of the Lower of Cost or Market (LCM) principle?","options":["FIFO","LIFO","Weighted Average","Specific Identification"],"answer":"B","explanation":"This answer is correct. This cost flow assumption uses Lower of Cost or Market.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.056_1805","question":"What is one disadvantage of the perpetual inventory system?","options":["It is less accurate than a periodic inventory system.","Companies require a longer operating cycle.","Companies must determine the cost of goods sold at the end of a period.","It requires more record keeping than a periodic inventory system."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Under a perpetual inventory system, the inventory account is updated whenever there is a transaction impacting inventory, specifically when inventory is purchased or sold. Cost of goods sold is determined for every sale at the time of the sale. Under this system, it is possible to know the inventory balance on any given day during the period. It is not necessary to conduct a physical count of inventory, although it can be used to determine if the inventory balance is correct. Under a periodic inventory system, a company determines cost of goods sold once at the end of the period. In addition, inventory purchases are debited to a “purchases” account. Since the inventory account is not updated during the period for purchases or sales, ending inventory needs to be determined by a physical inventory count. Although there are some advantages of a perpetual inventory system, it does require more record keeping than a periodic inventory system. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.inv.010_1802","question":"Which of the following inventory valuation methods cannot be used for IFRS purposes?","options":["FIFO","LIFO","Weighted Average","Specific Identification"],"answer":"B","explanation":"This answer is correct. This method cannot be used for IFRS purposes.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.002_1805","question":"Howard Incorporated is doing inventory. In the inventory process, Howard inadvertently miscategorized a $6,000 shipment of FOB destination goods in transit from a supplier as FOB shipping point. How will this miscategorization impact Howard's total inventory?","options":["Howard's total inventory will be $6,000 too low.","It will not impact Howard's total inventory.","Howard's total inventory will be $6,000 too high.","Howard's total inventory will be $3,000 too high."],"answer":"C","explanation":"When a shipment is categorized as FOB shipping point, the title to the goods changes hands when the goods leave the shipping point. The goods belong to the buyer while in transit. When a shipment is categorized as FOB destination, the title to the goods changes hands when the goods arrive at the destination. The goods belong to the seller while in transit. Howard categorizing the shipment as FOB shipping point when it should be FOB destination means it will mistakenly include the $6,000 in goods in its inventory while the shipment is in transit. This results in its inventory being $6,000 too high. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.015_1805","question":"Howe Corporation calculates inventory and cost of goods sold one time at the end of every accounting period. In contrast, Kelty Industries updates their inventory and cost of goods sold accounts multiple times in one day. What is the difference between Howe and Kelty?","options":["Howe uses a manual system of inventory, whereas Kelty uses an automatic system of inventory.","Howe uses a perpetual system of inventory, whereas Kelty uses a periodic system of inventory.","Howe uses a periodic system of inventory, whereas Kelty uses a perpetual system of inventory.","Howe uses an automatic system of inventory, whereas Kelty uses a manual system of inventory."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Under a periodic inventory system, cost of goods sold is determined at the end of the period. In addition, inventory purchases are debited to a “purchases” account. Since the inventory account is not updated during the period for purchases or sales, ending inventory needs to be determined by a physical inventory count. This is Howe's system. Under a perpetual inventory system, the inventory account is updated whenever there is a transaction impacting inventory (purchase or sale). Under this system, it is possible to know the inventory balance at any given time during the period. It is not necessary to conduct a physical count of inventory, although it can be used to determine if the inventory balance is correct. This is Kelty's system. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.009_1805","question":"Net sales are calculated by subtracting ____________ from __________.","options":["cost of goods sold; sales revenue","sales returns and allowances; sales revenue","sales revenue; sales returns and allowances","cost of goods sold; sales returns and allowances"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Net sales is defined as the amount of revenue expected to ultimately be received from a buyer. It is calculated as gross revenue less sales returns and allowances. Sales returns and allowances are defined as reductions in revenue (and accounts receivable) from returns and allowances provided to the buyer as compensation for incorrect or defective products being shipped. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.inv.002_1802","question":"The management accountant of Clifford Products has decided to value inventory using last in, first out (LIFO) to reduce its tax expense. Under which of the following situations can the decision of the management accountant go wrong?","options":["When the LIFO balance is the same as the FIFO balance due to stable costs.","When the prices are rising and inventory quantities on hand are decreasing.","When LIFO includes inventory holding gains in the net income.","When the company uses LIFO only for external financial reporting and average cost method for internal reporting."],"answer":"B","explanation":"This answer is correct. During rising prices, a reduction in inventory quantities results in higher levels of income because lower cost (older) inventories are being sold at higher prices, thereby increasing the tax expense. Hence, reducing inventory quantities can reduce the benefits of valuation of inventory using LIFO.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.inv.008_1802","question":"Warner Machines missed recording an end-of-year $10,000 inventory purchase on account in the current year's financial records. While finalizing the financial statements after the inventory was located during the year-end count, the company's accountant detected the error and attempted to correct it. Under which of the following situations will the company report lower than actual net income?","options":["The accountant has increased inventory and reduced cash by $10,000.","The accountant has increased cost of goods sold and increased accounts payable by $10,000.","The accountant has increased inventory and accounts payable by $10,000.","The accountant has reduced accounts payable and inventory by $10,000."],"answer":"B","explanation":"This answer is correct. When the company misses recording a purchase but includes the purchase as part of cost of goods sold (COGS) in the income statement, COGS will be overstated and the net income will be understated. The missing $10,000 should have been included both in ending inventory and in accounts payable, which would appropriately result in the COGS being unaffected. The net income is incorrect in this scenario.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.005_1805","question":"Which of the following is not included in product costs?","options":["Officer's salaries","Overhead","Material","Labor"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Product costs are defined as costs necessary to manufacture a product. They are capitalized as a part of inventory and only expensed when the goods are sold. Product costs consist of direct materials, direct labor, and manufacturing overhead. Officer's salaries are not a product cost. They are classified as period costs, meaning they are expensed in the period incurred. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.038_1805","question":"LIFO is used when prices are falling and companies wish to do which of the following?","options":["Maximize asset value","Minimize asset value","Maximize operating expenses","Minimize operating expenses"],"answer":"A","explanation":"LIFO uses the newest prices when calculating cost of goods sold and the oldest prices when calculating ending inventory. When prices are falling, ending inventory under LIFO is higher than under FIFO since it is calculated using older, higher prices. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.006_1805","question":"Joanie's Caterers bought 10 green tablecloths from Fabric Town for a reception planned for one of their clients. When the package arrived, there were 10 red tablecloths included, but no green. If Joanie's Caterers decides to keep the tablecloths, then:","options":["Fabric Town may grant a purchase allowance, which will increase the operating expenses for their company as well as Joanie's.","Fabric Town may grant a purchase return, which will increase accounts payable for Joanie's Caterers, but not Fabric Town.","Fabric Town may grant a purchase allowance, which will reduce the caterer's accounts payable.","Fabric Town may grant a purchase return, which will decrease accounts payable for Fabric Town, but not Joanie's Caterers."],"answer":"C","explanation":"When incorrect goods are delivered, sellers will often grant a discount to the buyer as compensation for the error. This reduction in price is called a purchase allowance. The purchase allowance reduces the amount owed by the buyer to the seller. This means the buyer's accounts payable will decrease. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.031_1805","question":"Companies hoping to minimize income tax expense will use which inventory cost flow assumption when prices are rising?","options":["FIFO","Average cost","Gross profit","LIFO"],"answer":"D","explanation":"LIFO uses the newest prices when calculating cost of goods sold and the oldest prices when calculating ending inventory. When prices are rising, cost of goods sold under LIFO is higher than under FIFO since it is calculated using newer, higher prices. When cost of goods sold under LIFO is higher, income before taxes is lower since net sales and all other expenses (other than cost of goods sold) are the same under LIFO and FIFO. Lower income before taxes results in lower income tax expense. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.059_1805","question":"Millennium Motors is a luxury car dealership. Which method of inventory costing would they most likely use?","options":["Specific identification","FIFO","LIFO","Weighted average"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The specific identification cost flow assumption tracks the costs of specific pieces of inventory. When that piece is sold, the costs assigned to that specific piece are expensed as cost of goods sold. Since a great deal of tracking is involved, this method is most appropriate with unique, valuable pieces of inventory. Luxury cars are unique and valuable pieces of inventory. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.032_1805","question":"During a period of rising prices, why would Paisley Inc. choose the LIFO inventory cost flow assumption?","options":["To track the exact profit made on each highly valuable piece of merchandise","To record the highest gross profit","To minimize income tax expense","To account for corresponding increases in the price of shipping to customers"],"answer":"C","explanation":"LIFO uses the newest prices when calculating cost of goods sold and the oldest prices when calculating ending inventory. When prices are rising, cost of goods sold under LIFO is higher than under FIFO since it is calculated using newer, higher prices. When cost of goods sold under LIFO is higher, income before taxes is lower since net sales and all other expenses (other than cost of goods sold) are the same under LIFO and FIFO. Lower income before taxes results in lower income tax expense. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.007_1805","question":"Ruby has purchased a set of handmade soaps and lotions from HandSpun Corp., which is located out of state. The goods are being shipped FOB destination. When did ownership of these soaps and lotions pass to Ruby?","options":["When they were ordered","When they were shipped","When they arrived","When they were in transit"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Shipping terms determine when ownership to goods changes hands. FOB destination means ownership changes when goods arrive at their destination. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.003_1805","question":"Where are the freight costs to sell goods included?","options":["As a selling expense","In the cost of inventory","As a type of overhead","In the cost of materials"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Freight costs on sales of inventory are considered selling expenses as it is considered a way of promoting sales. However, freight costs for inventory purchases are included in the cost of inventory as it is a necessary cost to get the asset (inventory) to the location of its intended use. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.043_1805","question":"Based on the net income of the shop, the sales staff at Francesca's Fashions receive performance bonuses. In periods of declining prices, which inventory costing method would bring the sales staff the most benefit?","options":["Average cost","FIFO","LIFO","Physical inventory method"],"answer":"C","explanation":"LIFO uses the newest prices when calculating cost of goods sold and the oldest prices when calculating ending inventory. When prices are declining, cost of goods sold under LIFO is lower than under FIFO since it is calculated using newer, lower prices. When cost of goods sold under LIFO is lower, net income is higher since net sales and all other expenses (other than cost of goods sold) are the same under FIFO and LIFO. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.018_1805","question":"Niles Industries is a merchandising company. It currently holds $6,000 in consignment goods for Martin Corporation. In addition, it has $4,000 in FOB shipping point goods in transit to Delta Enterprises and $5,000 in FOB destination goods in transit to Troy Manufacturing. Finally, Niles has $7,000 in FOB destination goods in transit from Gregg Supply. For which group of goods does Niles hold the title?","options":["The $4,000 in goods in transit to Delta Enterprises","The $6,000 in goods for Martin Corporation","The $5,000 in goods in transit to Troy Manufacturing","The $7,000 in goods in transit from Gregg Supply"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Goods a company holds on consignment are not legally owned by the company. These items are excluded from its inventory. For goods in transit, shipping terms determine the owner of the goods while they are in transit. Goods shipped FOB shipping point belong to the buyer while in transit as ownership passes to the buyer once the goods leave the shipping point. Goods shipped FOB destination belong to the seller while in transit as ownership does not pass to the buyer until the goods reach the destination. Niles owns the $5,000 in goods from Troy as ownership does not pass to Troy until the goods reach their destination. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.040_1805","question":"During a period of rising prices, why would Mariposa Corp. choose the FIFO inventory cost flow assumption?","options":["To track the exact profit made on each highly valuable piece of merchandise","To record the highest gross profit","To use the highest cost of goods sold to be conservative","To make the goods more attractive to buyers"],"answer":"B","explanation":"FIFO uses the oldest prices when calculating cost of goods sold and the newest prices when calculating ending inventory. When prices are rising, cost of goods sold under FIFO is lower than under LIFO since it is calculated using older, lower prices. When cost of goods sold under FIFO is lower, gross profit is higher since net sales are the same under FIFO and LIFO. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.inv.003_1802","question":"Sandra Bellucci, a financial analyst, is analyzing inventory of companies from four different industries: consumer goods, sports goods manufacturers, electronics, and aircraft manufacturers. Assuming that the inventory valuation methods reflect the actual flow of inventory and the inventory includes finished goods only, which of the following industries will most likely have similar costs under both FIFO and LIFO?","options":["Consumer goods","Sports goods manufacturers","Electronics","Aircraft manufacturers"],"answer":"D","explanation":"This answer is correct. Since this industry deals with high-value and customized orders, the production usually starts after the order is received. Since there will not be any equipment lying in inventory, the inventory balance will be zero, irrespective of the method of valuation used. Therefore, the inventory costing method would not create any differences in COGS.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.017_1805","question":"Badger Enterprises purchased aluminum from JG Metals. When Badger Enterprises recorded this transaction, they made entries into three accounts: Purchases, Freight Costs, and Purchase Discounts. Based on this, what type of inventory system does Badger Enterprises use?","options":["Perpetual system of inventory","Periodic system of inventory","Just-in-time system of inventory","Traditional system of inventory"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Under a periodic inventory system, a company determines cost of goods sold only once at the end of the period. In addition, inventory purchases and all associated costs of the purchase are included in the cost of the inventory. This includes freight charges, as GAAP requires all costs necessary to get an asset in condition and location for intended use be included in the cost of the asset. In addition, discounts for early payments (purchase discounts) reduce the cost of inventory as they reduce the amount paid for the goods. All of these items (purchases, freight costs, and purchase discounts) are kept in separate accounts until cost of goods sold is calculated at the end of the period. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.037_1805","question":"Companies that want to maximize the assets section of the balance sheet will use which inventory cost flow method during deflationary periods?","options":["FIFO","Average-cost","Specific identification","LIFO"],"answer":"D","explanation":"LIFO uses the newest prices when calculating cost of goods sold and the oldest prices when calculating ending inventory. When prices are declining, ending inventory under LIFO is higher than under FIFO since it is calculated using older, higher prices. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.055_1805","question":"The LIFO conformity rule requires companies using LIFO for tax purposes to do which of the following?","options":["Use FIFO for financial reporting purposes.","Use LIFO or FIFO for financial reporting.","Use LIFO for financial reporting purposes.","Use FIFO in the following tax year."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The IRS requires that firms using LIFO for tax purposes in a year also use LIFO for financial reporting purposes that year. This is called the LIFO conformity rule. The rule is to prevent firms from getting the lower taxable income that comes from using LIFO while reporting higher net income that comes from using FIFO (assuming prices are rising). Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.033_1805","question":"LIFO is used when prices are rising and companies wish to do which of the following?","options":["Maximize income taxes","Adjust beginning inventory","Minimize income taxes","Lower cost of goods"],"answer":"C","explanation":"LIFO uses the newest prices when calculating cost of goods sold and the oldest prices when calculating ending inventory. When prices are rising, cost of goods sold under LIFO is higher than under FIFO since it is calculated using newer, higher prices. When cost of goods sold under LIFO is higher, income before taxes is lower since net sales and all other expenses (other than cost of goods sold) are the same under LIFO and FIFO. Lower income before taxes results in lower income tax expense. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.030_1805","question":"Let A equal the reported inventory value if the lower-of-cost-or-NRV rule is applied to individual items of inventory, while B equals the reported inventory value if the lower-of-cost-or-NRV rule is applied to the inventory as a whole. In this situation, which of the following is true?","options":["A will always be equal to or less than B.","A will always be equal to B.","A will always be equal to or greater than B.","A will always be greater than B."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Applying the lower-of-cost-or-NRV rule to individual items is generally a more conservative approach than applying it to inventory as a whole. This is because when it is applied to inventory as a whole, items where NRV is higher than cost can offset items where NRV is lower than cost. This off-setting will not occur when it is applied to individual items. This results in the value when the lower of cost or NRV rule is applied to individual items always being less than or equal to (but never greater than) the value when it is applied to inventory as a whole. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.053_1805","question":"The Horton Corporation accepted the delivery on August 15, 20x4, of merchandise that it had purchased on account. As of August 31, Horton, which uses the periodic method, had not recorded the transaction or included the merchandise in its inventory count. What is the impact of this on their August 31, 20x4, balance sheet?","options":["The stockholders’ equity would be the only item affected by the omission.","The assets, liabilities, and stockholders' equity would be understated.","The assets and liabilities would be understated but stockholders’ equity would not be affected.","The assets and stockholders' equity would be overstated but liabilities would not be affected."],"answer":"C","explanation":"When merchandise is purchased under the periodic method the journal entry is a debit to “purchases” and a credit to “accounts payable.” Not making this entry understates liabilities since accounts payable is not increased as it should be. Not including the inventory in the inventory count at the end of the month also understates assets since inventory is too low. Not recording the purchase understates cost of goods available for sale since purchases are added to beginning inventory to determine it. Since ending inventory is understated by the same amount, cost of goods sold is correct. With cost of goods sold being correct, net income is correct. Since net income is closed into retained earnings, net income being correct means stockholders’ equity is correct. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.025_1805","question":"High-technology and fashion are types of industries likely to frequently use which of the following?","options":["The lower-of-cost-or-net-realizable-value basis","The average cost basis","The historical cost basis","Cost of goods sold"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The lower-of-cost-or-net-realizable-value basis must be applied when the market value of inventory falls below the historical cost of the inventory. High-technology firms are more likely than firms in other industries to use lower-of-cost-or-net-realizable-value because changes in technology can cause market prices to fall very quickly. Fashion industry firms are also more likely than firms in other industries to use lower-of-cost-or-net-realizable-value because changes in fashion tastes can cause market prices to fall very quickly. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.008_1805","question":"Ownership to goods shipped FOB destination is passed to the ________ when the goods ________.","options":["buyer; are ordered","buyer; are shipped","seller; are returned","buyer; arrive at the purchaser's business"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Shipping terms determine when ownership to goods changes hands. FOB destination means ownership passes to the buyer when goods arrive at the purchaser's location. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.041_1805","question":"In periods of rising prices, which inventory cost flow assumption will yield the highest gross profit?","options":["LIFO","Average cost","Specific identification","FIFO"],"answer":"D","explanation":"FIFO uses the oldest prices when calculating cost of goods sold and the newest prices when calculating ending inventory. When prices are rising, cost of goods sold under FIFO is lower than under LIFO since it is calculated using older, lower prices. When cost of goods sold under FIFO is lower, gross profit is higher since net sales are the same under FIFO and LIFO. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.029_1805","question":"At Line Drive Apparel, practice jerseys are sold for $30. The disposal costs are $5 per jersey. The historical cost is $22 per jersey but the current replacement cost is $20 per jersey. Line Drive Apparel's normal profit margin is 20% of the sale price. Using the lower-of-cost-or-net-realizable-value (NRV) comparison, what should be the inventory value of each jersey?","options":["$22","$19","$25","$20"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The NRV is calculated as selling price ($30) less selling costs ($5), which equals $25. This is more than the historical cost, so the inventory will still be valued at $22. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.042_1805","question":"Which of the following would change as a result of changing inventory cost flow assumptions?","options":["Gross profit on sales","Selling price","Sales discounts","Amount of sales"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A company must choose an inventory cost flow assumption to use when preparing financial statements. This choice impacts the calculation of cost of goods sold and ending inventory. Since the choice impacts cost of goods sold, it also impacts gross profit. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.057_1805","question":"What is one advantage of the periodic inventory system?","options":["It is more accurate than a perpetual inventory system.","Companies can keep a detailed daily record of inventory on hand.","It requires less record keeping than a perpetual inventory system.","Companies can determine the cost of goods sold each time a sale occurs."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Under a periodic inventory system, a company determines cost of goods sold once at the end of the period. In addition, inventory purchases are debited to a “purchases” account. Since the inventory account is not updated during the period for purchases or sales, ending inventory needs to be determined by a physical inventory count. Under a perpetual inventory system, the inventory account is updated whenever there is a transaction impacting inventory, specifically when inventory is purchased or sold. Cost of goods sold is determined for every sale at the time of the sale. Under this system, it is possible to know the inventory balance on any given day during the period. It is not necessary to conduct a physical count of inventory, although it can be used to determine if the inventory balance is correct. Because a perpetual inventory system updates inventory after every transaction while a periodic inventory system only updates it once per period, less record keeping is required under a periodic inventory system than under a perpetual inventory system. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.001_1805","question":"Which of the following would result in Walker Industries’ inventory being higher than it should be?","options":["Walker miscategorized FOB destination goods in transit to a customer as FOB shipping point.","Walker miscategorized FOB shipping point goods in transit from a supplier as FOB destination.","Walker miscategorized consignment goods in transit from a supplier as FOB destination.","Walker miscategorized FOB shipping point goods in transit to a customer as FOB destination."],"answer":"D","explanation":"When a shipment is categorized as FOB shipping point, the title to the goods changes hands when the goods leave the shipping point. The goods belong to the buyer while in transit. When a shipment is categorized as FOB destination, the title to the goods changes hands when the goods arrive at the destination. The goods belong to the seller while in transit. A seller categorizing a shipment as FOB destination when it should be FOB shipping point means the seller will mistakenly include the goods in its inventory while the shipment is in transit. This results in the seller's inventory being too high. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.054_1805","question":"By using a perpetual inventory system, companies can better track when to replenish inventory, thus reducing which of the following?","options":["Storage costs","Sales","Sales returns","Income"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Storage costs are incurred by companies to store inventory. As inventory increases, storage costs increase. Since perpetual inventory systems provide a “running total” of inventory on hand, companies are in a better position to know when to order inventory. Without that information, companies are likely to buy inventory earlier than needed and in larger amounts than needed to avoid stock-out costs. This increases storage costs. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.052_1805","question":"Merchandise that was purchased on account was accepted by the Orion Company. As of December 31, Orion had recorded the transaction using the periodic method, but did not include the merchandise in its inventory. What would be the effect of this on the financial statements for December 31?","options":["The net income, current assets, and retained earnings would all be understated.","The net income would be correct and current assets would be understated.","The net income would be understated and current liabilities would be overstated.","The net income would be overstated and current assets would be understated."],"answer":"A","explanation":"When merchandise is purchased under the periodic method the journal entry is a debit to “purchases” and a credit to “accounts payable.” Not including the purchase in inventory as of December 31 will understate Orion's current assets. Not including the inventory also results in an overstatement of cost of goods sold, since too little is subtracted from (correctly stated) cost of goods available for sale. The overstatement in cost of goods sold results in an understatement of net income. Since net income is closed into retained earnings, understating net income leads to understating retained earnings. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.024_1805","question":"What will be the effect on the financial statements in the year when slower-moving inventory items continue to be valued at historical cost instead of the lower of cost or net realizable value basis?","options":["Assets are understated and net income is overstated.","Assets are understated and net income is understated.","Assets are overstated and net income is overstated.","Assets are overstated and net income is understated."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The market value of slower-moving inventory items is often lower than their historical cost. If that is the case, then the inventory must be valued on a lower of cost or net realizable value basis. Not doing so will result in assets (inventory) being overstated, cost of goods sold being understated (as the impairment loss on inventory is frequently included in cost of goods sold), and net income being overstated. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.034_1805","question":"Companies wishing to minimize income taxes should use which inventory cost flow method during times when prices are declining?","options":["FIFO","LIFO","Average-cost","Periodic"],"answer":"A","explanation":"FIFO uses the oldest prices when calculating cost of goods sold and the newest prices when calculating ending inventory. When prices are declining, cost of goods sold under FIFO is higher than under LIFO since it is calculated using older, higher prices. When cost of goods sold under FIFO is higher, income before taxes is lower since net sales and all other expenses (other than cost of goods sold) are the same under FIFO and LIFO. Lower income before taxes results in lower income tax expense. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.inv.009_1802","question":"In a period of rising prices, which of the following inventory valuation methods will most likely have the lowest tax expense, all else equal?","options":["LIFO","FIFO","Weighted Average","Specific Identification"],"answer":"A","explanation":"This answer is correct. In a period of rising prices, LIFO expenses the most recent, and hence highest, costs. This results in higher COGS, lower income, and lower income tax expense.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.047_1805","question":"Which of the following errors will have no impact on the company's total inventory reported on the balance sheet?","options":["JT Engineering lists $7,500 worth of work-in-process inventory as finished goods inventory.","AJ Incorporated lists $13,600 worth of consignment goods as merchandise inventory.","DJ Enterprises lists $12,300 worth of FOB shipping point goods in transit to a customer as merchandise inventory.","RL Industries lists $8,700 worth of FOB destination goods in transit from a supplier as merchandise inventory."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Inventory on a manufacturer's balance sheet consists of raw materials, work-in-process, and finished goods. Incorrectly listing work-in-process inventory as finished goods inventory impacts the two individual components, but it has no impact on the total amount of inventory shown on the balance sheet. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.004_1805","question":"In addition to the purchase price of goods, the inventory account includes which cost?","options":["Freight costs to acquire the inventory","Freight costs to sell the inventory","Markups on inventory cost","Markdowns on selling price"],"answer":"A","explanation":"GAAP requires that the initial value of an asset include all the costs necessary to get the asset to the location and condition of its intended use. Freight costs for inventory purchases are included in the cost of inventory as it is a necessary cost to get the asset (inventory) to the location of its intended use. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.027_1805","question":"At Feline Fancy, the historical cost of the deluxe cat home is $40 and the retail price is $55. The company will spend $4 to sell the deluxe cat home and can replace it for $42. The company wishes to use the lower-of-cost-or-net-realizable-value comparison and has a profit margin is 30%. What number should they use for the net realizable value?","options":["$42.00","$40.00","$34.50","$51.00"],"answer":"D","explanation":"This is the selling price ($55) less the costs to sell ($4). This is the definition of net realizable value. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.014_1805","question":"Carla's company uses the periodic inventory system, and Carla is attempting to determine cost of goods sold. Her records show the company paid $12,000 in freight charges for the year ended December 31, but she is unsure whether to add or subtract this value. What should she do? Why?","options":["She should add freight charges because freight costs are considered part of the cost of purchasing inventory.","She should add freight charges because freight costs are reflected in the price the company charges its customers.","She should subtract freight charges because cost of goods should reflect only the value of the goods themselves.","She should subtract freight charges because they are paid by the seller of the goods and not the purchaser."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Under a periodic inventory system, a company determines cost of goods sold only once at the end of the period. In addition, inventory purchases and all associated costs of the purchase are included in the cost of the inventory. This includes freight charges, as GAAP requires all costs necessary to get an asset in condition and location for intended use be included in the cost of the asset. Since freight charges increase the cost of inventory, they must be added when calculating cost of goods sold. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.023_1805","question":"The acquisition cost of a heavily used raw material changes frequently. The inventory amount of this material at year end will be the same if perpetual records (units and costs) are kept as it would be under a periodic inventory method only if the inventory amount is computed under which of the following methods?","options":["Weighted-average method","Last-in, first-out method","First-in, first-out method","Direct costing method"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Under perpetual inventory cost of goods sold is calculated every time a sale is made. Under periodic inventory cost of goods sold is calculated only once at the end of the period. FIFO uses the oldest prices when calculating cost of goods sold and the newest prices when calculating ending inventory. Under FIFO, ending inventory is the same for perpetual and periodic inventory. This is because the oldest costs used to calculate cost of goods sold are the same whether cost of goods sold is calculated for every sale (perpetual) or once at the end of the period (periodic). If cost of goods sold is the same, ending inventory must be the same since the cost of goods available for sale is divided between ending inventory and cost of goods sold. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"aq.inv.001_1802","question":"Jonestown Company operates in an inflationary environment and sells luxury cars. Jonestown intends to decrease its tax burden by using LIFO for its inventory pricing method. Under which of the following circumstances would the tax-reducing implications of LIFO be mitigated?","options":["The products Jonestown sells are generally unaffected by inflationary pressure.","Jonestown indexes its prices to inflation.","Jonestown's competitors use specific identification of inventory.","The weighted average cost method produces costs that are typically 70% of the LIFO costs."],"answer":"A","explanation":"This answer is correct. If the luxury goods are not affected by the inflationary pressure, then the prices would generally be similar between FIFO and LIFO, reducing the tax implications of LIFO.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.045_1805","question":"The ending inventory of the Ryan Company is understated in year one by $20,000. This error is not corrected in year one or in year two. What impact will this error have on total net income for years one and two combined?","options":["No effect on total net income for the two years","Overstated total income by $20,000","Understated total income by $20,000","Overstated net income for year one by $20,000 and year two by $20,000 for a total overstatement of $40,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Understating ending inventory results in an overstatement of cost of goods sold for the year of the error since goods not included in the inventory balance are assumed to have been sold. This overstatement of cost of goods sold by $20,000 results in understating net income by $20,000 for the year of the error. If the error is not corrected, the beginning inventory for the next year is understated by $20,000. This results in an understatement of $20,000 in cost of goods sold for the next year since the amount of goods available for sale is too low. Assuming ending inventory for the next year is correct, understated cost of goods available for sale causes an understatement of cost of goods sold. If cost of goods sold for the next year is too low by $20,000, net income will be too high by $20,000. The understatement in net income of $20,000 in the year of the error is exactly offset by the overstatement in net income of $20,000 in the following year. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.048_1805","question":"Accurate Auditing is conducting an inventory count for Blake Industries. Blake intermingles empty boxes with full boxes in the storeroom. How would falsifying its inventory account have affected Blake's financial statements if the auditors had not discovered the fraud?","options":["Assets would have been understated.","Liabilities would have been understated.","Assets would have been overstated.","Revenues would have been overstated."],"answer":"C","explanation":"If an empty box is mistaken for a full box when inventory is counted, total inventory will be overstated. If inventory is overstated, then total assets would be overstated. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.058_1805","question":"What is one disadvantage of the perpetual inventory system?","options":["It provides more control over inventories than a periodic inventory system.","It requires more record keeping than a periodic inventory system.","Companies can keep a detailed daily record of inventory on hand.","Companies can determine the cost of goods sold each time a sale occurs."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Under a perpetual inventory system, the inventory account is updated whenever there is a transaction impacting inventory, specifically when inventory is purchased or sold. Cost of goods sold is determined for every sale at the time of the sale. Under this system, it is possible to know the inventory balance on any given day during the period. It is not necessary to conduct a physical count of inventory, although it can be used to determine if the inventory balance is correct. Under a periodic inventory system, a company determines cost of goods sold only once at the end of the period. In addition, inventory purchases are debited to a “purchases” account. Since the inventory account is not updated during the period for purchases or sales, ending inventory needs to be determined by a physical inventory count. Although there are some advantages of a perpetual inventory system, a disadvantage of the system is that it does require more record keeping than a periodic inventory system. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.019_1805","question":"Assuming no beginning inventory, what is the trend of inventory prices if cost of goods sold computed when inventory is valued using the FIFO method exceeds cost of goods sold when inventory is valued using the LIFO method?","options":["Prices will remain unchanged.","Prices will decrease.","Prices will increase.","The price trend cannot be determined from the information given."],"answer":"B","explanation":"FIFO uses the oldest prices when calculating cost of goods sold and the newest prices when calculating ending inventory. On the other hand, LIFO uses the newest prices when calculating cost of goods sold and the oldest prices when calculating ending inventory. If cost of goods sold under FIFO is higher than cost of goods sold under LIFO, older prices must be higher than newer prices. In other words, prices have decreased. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.016_1805","question":"What is one primary difference between determining cost of goods sold with a periodic system rather than a perpetual system?","options":["With a periodic system, cost of goods sold is calculated only once per day. In contrast, with a perpetual system, cost of goods sold is recorded each time a sale is made.","With a periodic system, cost of goods sold is recorded each time a sale is made. In contrast, with a perpetual system, cost of goods sold is only calculated once at the end of the period.","With a periodic system, cost of goods sold is calculated only once at the end of the period. In contrast, with a perpetual system, cost of goods sold is recorded each time a sale is made.","With a periodic system, cost of goods sold is recorded each time a sale is made. In contrast, with a perpetual system, cost of goods sold is only calculated once per day."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Under a periodic inventory system, cost of goods sold is determined at the end of the period only. This is because a physical count of inventory is needed for this calculation. Under a perpetual inventory system, the inventory account is updated whenever there is a transaction impacting inventory (purchase or sale). This means cost of goods sold is determined for every sale at the time of the sale. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.044_1805","question":"Global Industries is a merchandising company that sells goods on consignment. Currently, Global has $14,000 worth of consignment goods in its warehouse that it included in its inventory. What effect will this have on Global's total inventory?","options":["Inventory will be $14,000 lower than expected.","Inventory will be calculated correctly.","Inventory will be $14,000 higher than expected.","Inventory will be $7,000 higher than expected."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Goods a company holds on consignment are not legally owned by the company. These items should be excluded from its inventory. If Global includes goods on consignment in its inventory, then its inventory will be overstated by the full value of the inventory. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.011_1805","question":"Gadget Corp., a wholesaler, uses a periodic inventory system. At year-end, how does Gadget Corp. determine its ending inventory?","options":["By conducting a physical count of inventory","By calculating inventory purchases for the year","By calculating inventory sales for the year","By subtracting income sales from beginning inventory"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Under a periodic inventory system, a company determines cost of goods sold only once at the end of the period. In addition, inventory purchases are debited to a “purchases” account. Since the inventory account is not updated during the period for purchases or sales, ending inventory needs to be determined by a physical inventory count. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.051_1805","question":"The Preston Company received merchandise on consignment. Preston included the goods in inventory as of April 30, but did not record the transaction. What would the effect of this be on its financial statements for April 30?","options":["The net income would be correct and the current assets would be understated.","The net income, current assets, and retained earnings would all be overstated.","The net income and current assets would be overstated and current liabilities would be understated.","The net income, current assets, and retained earnings would all be understated."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Goods a company holds on consignment are not legally owned by the company. These items should be excluded from its inventory. If Preston includes the goods in its inventory on April 30, current assets are overstated since Preston does not legally own them. Since Preston did not record the receipt of goods (correctly), cost of goods available for sale is correctly stated. Since ending inventory is overstated, cost of goods sold is understated. Since cost of goods sold is understated, net income is overstated. Since net income is closed into retained earnings, overstating net income leads to overstating retained earnings. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.049_1805","question":"The beginning inventory for 20x4 is overstated. The effects of this error on cost of goods sold for 20x4, net income for 20x4, and assets at December 31, 20x4, respectively, are represented by which of the following options?","options":["Overstatement, understatement, overstatement","Understatement, overstatement, overstatement","Overstatement, understatement, no effect","Understatement, overstatement, no effect"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Overstating beginning inventory for 20x4 results in an overstatement of cost of goods available for sale in 20x4. The overstatement of cost of goods available for sale in 20x4 results in an overstatement of cost of goods sold for 20x4 since ending inventory is subtracted from cost of goods available for sale to determine cost of goods sold. Net income for 20x4 is understated because of the overstatement of cost of goods sold in 20x4. There is no effect on inventory as of December 31, 20x4, since the ending inventory is determined by an inventory count. Since there is no effect on inventory, there is no effect on assets. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.039_1805","question":"New Haven Products has used LIFO inventory valuation for the life of the company. Each year, their LIFO reserve increases. What can be assumed about the prices of inventory?","options":["Inventory prices have increased each year.","Inventory prices have decreased each year.","Inventory prices have stayed the same each year.","Inventory prices have increased in more years than they have decreased."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The LIFO reserve measures the difference between ending inventory under LIFO and FIFO. LIFO uses the newest prices for calculating COGS and the oldest prices for calculating ending inventory. In the opposite way, FIFO uses the oldest prices for calculating COGS and the newest prices for calculating ending inventory. If the LIFO reserve increases every year, it means that the difference between ending inventory increases every year. This happens when prices are increasing because LIFO uses older (lower) prices to calculate ending inventory and FIFO uses newer (higher) prices to calculate ending inventory. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.010_1805","question":"Pristine Products, a wholesaler, uses a periodic inventory system. At year-end, Pristine conducts a physical inventory count to determine which of the following?","options":["Inventory purchases for the year","Source of discrepancies in inventory records","Beginning inventory","Ending inventory"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Under a periodic inventory system, a company determines cost of goods sold only once at the end of the period. In addition, inventory purchases are debited to a “purchases” account. Cost of goods sold is determined as “Beginning inventory + Net purchases – Ending inventory.” Since the inventory account is not updated during the period, ending inventory needs to be determined by a physical inventory count. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.046_1805","question":"Which of the following demonstrates the results of failure to record a purchase of merchandise on account even though the goods are properly included in the physical inventory? Assume the periodic method is used.","options":["An understatement of assets and net income","An understatement of cost of goods sold and liabilities and an overstatement of assets","An understatement of liabilities and an overstatement of owners' equity","An overstatement of assets and net income"],"answer":"C","explanation":"When merchandise is purchased under the periodic method the journal entry is a debit to “purchases” and a credit to “accounts payable.” Not making this entry results in liabilities being understated. Not making the entry also results in an understatement of cost of goods available sale, which results in an understatement of cost of goods sold (assuming ending inventory is correct). The understatement in cost of goods sold results in an overstatement of income, which results in an overstatement of owners’ equity. Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0},{"id":"tb.inv.028_1805","question":"At Line Drive Apparel, practice jerseys are sold for $30. The disposal costs are $5 per jersey. The historical cost is $22 per jersey but the current replacement cost is $20 per jersey. Line Drive Apparel's normal profit margin is 20% of the sale price. What is the net realizable value of each jersey that Line Drive Apparel should use in the lower-of-cost-or-net-realizable-value (NRV) comparison?","options":["$20","$22","$19","$25"],"answer":"D","explanation":"NRV is equal to selling price less costs to sell. In this example, the net realizable value is $25 ($30 − $5).Therefore, this is the correct answer.","unit":2,"subunit":"2.2","volume":"supplemental","source":"Supplemental answer-source documents","questionNumber":0}];